{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Vrd1h3xGpRh5FTywKmX3G9","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tRESEARCH ORCHESTRATOR (UNIFIED: KB + WEB)\n2\t\n3\tYou will complete a one-pass research workflow using local KB and/or external sources. Do not invent citations.\n4\t\n5\tInputs (absolute paths provided):\n6\t- TASK_FILE: {{TASK_FILE}}\n7\t- KB_DIR (optional): {{KB_DIR}}\n8\t- SESSION_DIR: {{SESSION_DIR}}\n9\t- FORMAT_RULES: {{FORMAT_RULES_PATH}}\n10\t- CITATIONS_RULES: {{CITATIONS_RULES_PATH}}\n11\t- AUDIT_RULES: {{AUDIT_RULES_PATH}}\n12\t- MANIFEST: {{SESSION_DIR}}/manifest.json   # CLI-provided manifest with KB documents + outputs\n13\t\n14\tGoal:\n15\tProduce ONE final publication-ready document saved to the manifest-defined output:\n16\t- Read MANIFEST first.\n17\t- Write the final manuscript to:\n18\t  {{SESSION_DIR}}/<MANIFEST.outputs.primary>\n19\tDo NOT derive output filenames from the workflow filename or from TASK_FILE.\n20\t\n21\tStructured prompts and slots:\n22\t- If TASK_FILE contains `## SLOTS` and `## CONSTRAINTS`, treat it as a structured prompt produced by the `prompt` workflow.\n23\t- Parse the slot headings (e.g., `### SLOT 1: ...`, `### SLOT 2: ...`) and treat each slot as a separate top-level section in your output.\n24\t- For each slot, answer only that slot's instructions within its section; do not mix content from different slots.\n25\t- When TASK_FILE constraints include phrases like \"No bullet points anywhere in the document\" or \"No subheadings or subsections within slots\", you MUST obey them strictly:\n26\t  * Do not use bullet lists; write only flowing paragraphs.\n27\t  * Inside each slot section, do not introduce additional `###` subheadings or nested headings; keep only the slot heading and paragraphs.\n28\t\n29\tMethod:\n30\t1) KB scan / targeted extraction (if KB_DIR provided):\n31\t   - Read TASK_FILE to understand scope, questions, and any explicit references\n32\t     to KB documents (e.g. `Documents: foo.md, bar.md` or file names mentioned\n33\t     in SLOTS/CONSTRAINTS).\n34\t   - Read MANIFEST once from {{SESSION_DIR}}/manifest.json and parse its\n35\t     `source.documents` and `outputs` fields conceptually (no need to\n36\t     rewrite the JSON).\n37\t   - Treat `source.documents` as the complete list of candidate KB `.md` files\n38\t     under KB_DIR; you MUST NOT perform any additional Glob/ls sobre KB_DIR.\n39\t   - Do **NOT** blindly read the full contents of every `.md` file:\n40\t     * Prefer documents that are explicitly referenced in TASK_FILE (by\n41\t       filename or via `Documents:`) for the current slot.\n42\t     * Only read a KB file when you actually need information from it.\n43\t   - When reading a KB file, you MUST respect context/token limits:\n44\t     * Never load more than a safe slice (e.g. a few thousand tokens) from a\n45\t       single file in a single read operation.\n46\t     * For large `.md` files, first skim structure (headings, table of\n47\t       contents, section titles) using a small prefix of the file.\n48\t     * Then, read only the specific sections you need (by heading/line ranges),\n49\t       instead of the entire file at once.\n50\t   - If MANIFEST.source.documents is empty, STOP and emit an explicit error.\n51\t   - Note: PDF/DOCX files must be pre-processed with the `retrieve` workflow\n52\t     first into `.md` under KB_DIR.\n53\t   - From the subset of actually-read KB content, extract facts, parameters,\n54\t     tables, methods, definitions, and quantitative results.\n55\t   - Type each statement: (a) factual/parametric, (b) evaluative/benchmark,\n56\t     (c) methodological.\n57\t\n58\t2) External research pass (WEB + ZOTERO):\n59\t   - After using the KB to answer the TASK_FILE as far as possible, perform an\n60\t     external research pass on those questions or subtopics where the KB does\n61\t     not fully answer the task or where the TASK_FILE explicitly requests\n62\t     external references or benchmarks.\n63\t   - Build short, focused queries that capture each question/subtopic.\n64\t   - For each query:\n65\t     * Search the web and fetch authoritative sources using whatever browsing\n66\t       capability is available in your runtime.\n67\t     * If a Zotero integration/library is available, query it with the **same**\n68\t       search string to obtain canonical metadata (title, year, DOI/URL).\n69\t   - Preference ladder for citations in the final manuscript:\n70\t     DOI > ARXIV > institutional WEB (avoid generic non-peer-reviewed sites).\n71\t   - When Zotero returns a reliable DOI/URL, use it as the primary source\n72\t     ([DOI:...]/[WEB:...]) and optionally add `[ZOTERO:item_key]` to mark\n73\t     that it also exists in the local library.\n74\t\n75\t3) Draft assembly:\n76\t   - Assemble complete draft answering TASK_FILE questions based on steps (1)-(2)\n77\t   - Place inline tokens at sentence end:\n78\t     * [KB:filename.md] for KB sources (only .md files)\n79\t     * [DOI:...] / [ARXIV:...] / [WEB:...] for external sources\n80\t     * [ZOTERO:item_key] for Zotero library sources (if used)\n81\t\n82\t4) Post-processing (apply rules):\n83\t  - Apply FORMAT_RULES, CITATIONS_RULES, and AUDIT_RULES (provided as system instructions).\n84\t  - Apply FORMAT_RULES to the draft to enforce math notation, professional tone, character-level hygiene, and de-duplication as specified there (do not add structural conventions beyond what the TASK_FILE and KB already imply).\n85\t  - Apply CITATIONS_RULES to enforce citation token types, placement, and coverage.\n86\t  - If a cited KB passage you retain already contains visible `[@Key]` citekeys, preserve those `[@Key]` citekeys verbatim adjacent to the retained claim; do not collapse them to a bare `[KB:filename.md]` token only.\n87\t  - Append paragraph-level audit footnotes using AUDIT_RULES (footnote format, hallucination taxonomy, and confidence levels).\n88\t  - Standalone display math blocks (for example `$$...$$`) are not paragraphs for audit purposes, but any prose paragraph that introduces or interprets them still needs its own audit footnote before the blank line.\n89\t  - Mandatory final block sweep before save:\n90\t    * Re-read the full draft as blank-line-separated Markdown blocks.\n91\t    * Every prose block MUST already end with exactly one `^[Confidence: ..., Rationale: ...]` footnote before the next blank line.\n92\t    * A prose block that ends with a colon before a display equation is still a prose block and MUST keep its own footnote; do not let a later footnote after the equation “cover” it.\n93\t    * Correct pattern: `Lead-in claim...^[Confidence: ...]` + blank line + `$$...$$` + blank line + `Interpretation...^[Confidence: ...]`.\n94\t    * Do NOT claim success until this sweep finds zero prose blocks without audit footnotes.\n95\t  - Evidence rule for the audit step in this workflow: Base your audit judgment only on the paragraph's content and its in-text citations present in the document (`[KB:/WEB:/DOI:/ARXIV:/ZOTERO:]`). Do not invent sources or fetch new ones during this audit step. If evidence is insufficient or ambiguous, your `Rationale` MUST say so explicitly and you SHOULD set `Confidence: LOW`. Treat KB documents as authoritative for this audit step.\n96\t  - Citation style in this workflow: KEEP token citations visible in the final output ([KB:filename.md], [DOI:...], [ARXIV:...], [WEB:...], [ZOTERO:...]); do not convert to [@key] unless explicitly requested; build REFERENCES.BIB only if [@key] citations are present.\n97\t  - If any file cannot be read, STOP and emit an explicit error noting which path failed.\n98\t\n99\t5) Write final manuscript:\n100\t   - Read MANIFEST and treat `outputs.primary` as the only valid output filename.\n101\t   - Save/write the full document to: {{SESSION_DIR}}/<MANIFEST.outputs.primary>\n102\t   - File must contain full manuscript with all content, citations, and audit footnotes\n103\t   - After saving, confirm: \"Manuscript written to {{SESSION_DIR}}/<MANIFEST.outputs.primary>\"\n104\t\n105\tExternal capabilities (if available):\n106\t- Web browsing/search: MUST be used as part of the external research pass to extend the answer beyond the KB (find additional authoritative sources, fill aspects that KB does not cover, and provide benchmarks explicitly requested in TASK_FILE).\n107\t- Zotero integration/library: best-effort helper. This workflow MUST always\n108\t  attempt Zotero lookups as described above when available, but it MUST NOT\n109\t  depend on them to complete; Zotero unavailability MUST be treated as\n110\t  non-fatal, skipping Zotero results and relying on KB + web browsing/search\n111\t  only.\n112\t- Orchestrator memory tool: when available, MUST be used as shared cache for web search results to avoid repeating searches already done by other workers. BEFORE performing any WebSearch, check memory for prior results on the same topic. After finding any new web result, store a brief summary with the source URL in memory. Do NOT cite memory as evidence; always cite the underlying sources as `[WEB:...]` / `[DOI:...]` / `[ARXIV:...]`.\n113\t\n114\tConstraints:\n115\t- Read ONLY .md files from KB_DIR (PDF/DOCX must be pre-processed with retrieve first)\n116\t- Do NOT call MCP helpers other than optional Zotero and an optional orchestrator memory tool used strictly as shared cache/state.\n117\t- If you include Markdown code blocks in the output, treat them as opaque: do not place citations or audit footnotes inside the block; place any required audit footnote immediately after the closing fence.\n118\t- Prioritize KB (if provided); use externals for gaps, validation, benchmarks\n119\t- MUST create the output file; do not just emit text\n120\t- Do not invent citations. Use only sources from KB, Zotero, or discovered via web browsing/search\n121\t- Keep citations in the `[KB:...]`, `[DOI:...]`, `[ARXIV:...]`, `[WEB:...]`, `[ZOTERO:...]` token form; do not convert any citation to [@Key].\n122\t- Preserve existing visible `[@Key]` citekeys that already appear in retained KB passages.\n123\t- Do not create any separate bibliography file in this workflow (no `biblatex.bib` here).\n124\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7d52a3ef-833c-4d3d-a19f-380d6d5297be","uuid":"15d4fd2f-7e2b-4caf-a60e-f1b0219f281f","timestamp":"2026-07-06T17:09:56.152Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/usr/local/libexec/tito/workflows/research.session.md","content":"RESEARCH ORCHESTRATOR (UNIFIED: KB + WEB)\n\nYou will complete a one-pass research workflow using local KB and/or external sources. Do not invent citations.\n\nInputs (absolute paths provided):\n- TASK_FILE: {{TASK_FILE}}\n- KB_DIR (optional): {{KB_DIR}}\n- SESSION_DIR: {{SESSION_DIR}}\n- FORMAT_RULES: {{FORMAT_RULES_PATH}}\n- CITATIONS_RULES: {{CITATIONS_RULES_PATH}}\n- AUDIT_RULES: {{AUDIT_RULES_PATH}}\n- MANIFEST: {{SESSION_DIR}}/manifest.json   # CLI-provided manifest with KB documents + outputs\n\nGoal:\nProduce ONE final publication-ready document saved to the manifest-defined output:\n- Read MANIFEST first.\n- Write the final manuscript to:\n  {{SESSION_DIR}}/<MANIFEST.outputs.primary>\nDo NOT derive output filenames from the workflow filename or from TASK_FILE.\n\nStructured prompts and slots:\n- If TASK_FILE contains `## SLOTS` and `## CONSTRAINTS`, treat it as a structured prompt produced by the `prompt` workflow.\n- Parse the slot headings (e.g., `### SLOT 1: ...`, `### SLOT 2: ...`) and treat each slot as a separate top-level section in your output.\n- For each slot, answer only that slot's instructions within its section; do not mix content from different slots.\n- When TASK_FILE constraints include phrases like \"No bullet points anywhere in the document\" or \"No subheadings or subsections within slots\", you MUST obey them strictly:\n  * Do not use bullet lists; write only flowing paragraphs.\n  * Inside each slot section, do not introduce additional `###` subheadings or nested headings; keep only the slot heading and paragraphs.\n\nMethod:\n1) KB scan / targeted extraction (if KB_DIR provided):\n   - Read TASK_FILE to understand scope, questions, and any explicit references\n     to KB documents (e.g. `Documents: foo.md, bar.md` or file names mentioned\n     in SLOTS/CONSTRAINTS).\n   - Read MANIFEST once from {{SESSION_DIR}}/manifest.json and parse its\n     `source.documents` and `outputs` fields conceptually (no need to\n     rewrite the JSON).\n   - Treat `source.documents` as the complete list of candidate KB `.md` files\n     under KB_DIR; you MUST NOT perform any additional Glob/ls sobre KB_DIR.\n   - Do **NOT** blindly read the full contents of every `.md` file:\n     * Prefer documents that are explicitly referenced in TASK_FILE (by\n       filename or via `Documents:`) for the current slot.\n     * Only read a KB file when you actually need information from it.\n   - When reading a KB file, you MUST respect context/token limits:\n     * Never load more than a safe slice (e.g. a few thousand tokens) from a\n       single file in a single read operation.\n     * For large `.md` files, first skim structure (headings, table of\n       contents, section titles) using a small prefix of the file.\n     * Then, read only the specific sections you need (by heading/line ranges),\n       instead of the entire file at once.\n   - If MANIFEST.source.documents is empty, STOP and emit an explicit error.\n   - Note: PDF/DOCX files must be pre-processed with the `retrieve` workflow\n     first into `.md` under KB_DIR.\n   - From the subset of actually-read KB content, extract facts, parameters,\n     tables, methods, definitions, and quantitative results.\n   - Type each statement: (a) factual/parametric, (b) evaluative/benchmark,\n     (c) methodological.\n\n2) External research pass (WEB + ZOTERO):\n   - After using the KB to answer the TASK_FILE as far as possible, perform an\n     external research pass on those questions or subtopics where the KB does\n     not fully answer the task or where the TASK_FILE explicitly requests\n     external references or benchmarks.\n   - Build short, focused queries that capture each question/subtopic.\n   - For each query:\n     * Search the web and fetch authoritative sources using whatever browsing\n       capability is available in your runtime.\n     * If a Zotero integration/library is available, query it with the **same**\n       search string to obtain canonical metadata (title, year, DOI/URL).\n   - Preference ladder for citations in the final manuscript:\n     DOI > ARXIV > institutional WEB (avoid generic non-peer-reviewed sites).\n   - When Zotero returns a reliable DOI/URL, use it as the primary source\n     ([DOI:...]/[WEB:...]) and optionally add `[ZOTERO:item_key]` to mark\n     that it also exists in the local library.\n\n3) Draft assembly:\n   - Assemble complete draft answering TASK_FILE questions based on steps (1)-(2)\n   - Place inline tokens at sentence end:\n     * [KB:filename.md] for KB sources (only .md files)\n     * [DOI:...] / [ARXIV:...] / [WEB:...] for external sources\n     * [ZOTERO:item_key] for Zotero library sources (if used)\n\n4) Post-processing (apply rules):\n  - Apply FORMAT_RULES, CITATIONS_RULES, and AUDIT_RULES (provided as system instructions).\n  - Apply FORMAT_RULES to the draft to enforce math notation, professional tone, character-level hygiene, and de-duplication as specified there (do not add structural conventions beyond what the TASK_FILE and KB already imply).\n  - Apply CITATIONS_RULES to enforce citation token types, placement, and coverage.\n  - If a cited KB passage you retain already contains visible `[@Key]` citekeys, preserve those `[@Key]` citekeys verbatim adjacent to the retained claim; do not collapse them to a bare `[KB:filename.md]` token only.\n  - Append paragraph-level audit footnotes using AUDIT_RULES (footnote format, hallucination taxonomy, and confidence levels).\n  - Standalone display math blocks (for example `$$...$$`) are not paragraphs for audit purposes, but any prose paragraph that introduces or interprets them still needs its own audit footnote before the blank line.\n  - Mandatory final block sweep before save:\n    * Re-read the full draft as blank-line-separated Markdown blocks.\n    * Every prose block MUST already end with exactly one `^[Confidence: ..., Rationale: ...]` footnote before the next blank line.\n    * A prose block that ends with a colon before a display equation is still a prose block and MUST keep its own footnote; do not let a later footnote after the equation “cover” it.\n    * Correct pattern: `Lead-in claim...^[Confidence: ...]` + blank line + `$$...$$` + blank line + `Interpretation...^[Confidence: ...]`.\n    * Do NOT claim success until this sweep finds zero prose blocks without audit footnotes.\n  - Evidence rule for the audit step in this workflow: Base your audit judgment only on the paragraph's content and its in-text citations present in the document (`[KB:/WEB:/DOI:/ARXIV:/ZOTERO:]`). Do not invent sources or fetch new ones during this audit step. If evidence is insufficient or ambiguous, your `Rationale` MUST say so explicitly and you SHOULD set `Confidence: LOW`. Treat KB documents as authoritative for this audit step.\n  - Citation style in this workflow: KEEP token citations visible in the final output ([KB:filename.md], [DOI:...], [ARXIV:...], [WEB:...], [ZOTERO:...]); do not convert to [@key] unless explicitly requested; build REFERENCES.BIB only if [@key] citations are present.\n  - If any file cannot be read, STOP and emit an explicit error noting which path failed.\n\n5) Write final manuscript:\n   - Read MANIFEST and treat `outputs.primary` as the only valid output filename.\n   - Save/write the full document to: {{SESSION_DIR}}/<MANIFEST.outputs.primary>\n   - File must contain full manuscript with all content, citations, and audit footnotes\n   - After saving, confirm: \"Manuscript written to {{SESSION_DIR}}/<MANIFEST.outputs.primary>\"\n\nExternal capabilities (if available):\n- Web browsing/search: MUST be used as part of the external research pass to extend the answer beyond the KB (find additional authoritative sources, fill aspects that KB does not cover, and provide benchmarks explicitly requested in TASK_FILE).\n- Zotero integration/library: best-effort helper. This workflow MUST always\n  attempt Zotero lookups as described above when available, but it MUST NOT\n  depend on them to complete; Zotero unavailability MUST be treated as\n  non-fatal, skipping Zotero results and relying on KB + web browsing/search\n  only.\n- Orchestrator memory tool: when available, MUST be used as shared cache for web search results to avoid repeating searches already done by other workers. BEFORE performing any WebSearch, check memory for prior results on the same topic. After finding any new web result, store a brief summary with the source URL in memory. Do NOT cite memory as evidence; always cite the underlying sources as `[WEB:...]` / `[DOI:...]` / `[ARXIV:...]`.\n\nConstraints:\n- Read ONLY .md files from KB_DIR (PDF/DOCX must be pre-processed with retrieve first)\n- Do NOT call MCP helpers other than optional Zotero and an optional orchestrator memory tool used strictly as shared cache/state.\n- If you include Markdown code blocks in the output, treat them as opaque: do not place citations or audit footnotes inside the block; place any required audit footnote immediately after the closing fence.\n- Prioritize KB (if provided); use externals for gaps, validation, benchmarks\n- MUST create the output file; do not just emit text\n- Do not invent citations. Use only sources from KB, Zotero, or discovered via web browsing/search\n- Keep citations in the `[KB:...]`, `[DOI:...]`, `[ARXIV:...]`, `[WEB:...]`, `[ZOTERO:...]` token form; do not convert any citation to [@Key].\n- Preserve existing visible `[@Key]` citekeys that already appear in retained KB passages.\n- Do not create any separate bibliography file in this workflow (no `biblatex.bib` here).\n","numLines":124,"startLine":1,"totalLines":124}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01VsW8SVhReSXUpqWDY37YcV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_011jsm7DQ1C5i9TWLYdjHpu7","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/usr/local/libexec/tito/rules/format.session.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":18984,"cache_read_input_tokens":16937,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":18984,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7d52a3ef-833c-4d3d-a19f-380d6d5297be","uuid":"2640a85b-44cc-4bb4-b933-860de59ec8de","request_id":"req_011CcmB3WLCqt8LVZofCZgaW"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011jsm7DQ1C5i9TWLYdjHpu7","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tPROFESSIONAL DOCUMENT STYLE FORMATTING\n2\t\n3\tPurpose: Transform technical documents into publication-ready professional format.\n4\t\n5\tIMPORTANT: BEFORE FORMATTING - ANALYZE DOCUMENT STATE\n6\t\n7\tPRE-FORMAT ANALYSIS REQUIREMENTS:\n8\t1. **Check Current Format**: Assess if the document already meets professional clarity and consistency\n9\t2. **Mathematical Notation Check**: Verify if equations use proper LaTeX format ($...$, $$...$$) where applicable\n10\t3. **Citation Format**: Ensure citations follow project token policy or normalized [@key]\n11\t4. **Quality Assessment**: Determine if the document exhibits publication-ready professional characteristics\n12\t5. **De-duplication and Consistency**: Detect duplicated content and potential contradictions; resolve when possible\n13\t\n14\tQUALITY TARGETS (NON-GATING):\n15\t- Clear organization suitable for a professional technical audience\n16\t- Mathematical expressions (if present) in LaTeX format ($...$ or `$$...$$`)\n17\t- Consistent, normalized citations (project tokens or [@key])\n18\t- Professional/academic voice and consistent technical terminology\n19\t\n20\tNote: These targets are mandatory goals to achieve. Always apply all formatting checks regardless of perceived document quality.\n21\t\n22\tMATHEMATICAL EXPRESSIONS:\n23\t\n24\tInline Math ($...$ format):\n25\t- Use for mathematical symbols, variables, and simple expressions within text\n26\t- Examples: $x$, $f(x)$, $n > 100$, $\\alpha = 0.05$, $O(n \\log n)$\n27\t- Use for brief formulas: $E[X] = \\mu$, $\\beta_1 = \\mu_3^2/\\mu_2^3$\n28\t- Single-line expressions that flow with text must be short: $Z = \\gamma + \\delta f((X - \\xi)/\\lambda)$\n29\t\n30\tDisplay Math ($$...$$ format):\n31\t- Use for standalone equations that deserve emphasis or are complex\n32\t- Multi-line expressions, definitions, theorems\n33\t- Integration/summation with multiple terms: $$H[f] = -\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f(x) \\log f(x) dx$$\n34\t- Matrix expressions, optimization problems\n35\t- Equations with fractions, large operators, or multiple levels: $$\\kappa = \\frac{\\beta_1(\\beta_2+3)^2}{4(4\\beta_2-3\\beta_1)(2\\beta_2-3\\beta_1-6)}$$\n36\t- Piecewise functions, case definitions\n37\t\n38\tDisplay Math Promotion Gate:\n39\t- Before final save, scan every inline `$...$` math span.\n40\t- Convert an inline span to a standalone `$$...$$` display block when the\n41\t  span is a complete equation or definition and contains any of these\n42\t  display triggers: `\\int`, `\\sum`, `\\prod`, `\\lim`, `\\max`, `\\min`,\n43\t  `\\frac{`, `\\sqrt{`, `\\left`, `\\right`, `\\begin{`, `cases`, `matrix`,\n44\t  `aligned`.\n45\t- Convert named engineering index or ratio definitions to display math when\n46\t  the equation is the object being defined, for example peak/RMS/effective\n47\t  strain measures, CSR, MSF, LPI, LSN, FOS/FS, PGA-derived demand, or\n48\t  pore-pressure ratio definitions.\n49\t- Keep inline math for short symbols, units, variable references, and compact\n50\t  relations that are incidental to the sentence.\n51\t- Do not duplicate the equation: promote by moving the equation to display\n52\t  form and keep the surrounding prose as prose.\n53\t\n54\tFORBIDDEN Math Formats:\n55\t- Never use \\(...\\) or \\[...\\]\n56\t- No Unicode symbols in equations (β, α, ∑, ∫)\n57\t- No inconsistent notation (use $\\lambda$ throughout, not switching to $l$)\n58\t- No equations in code blocks unless showing actual code\n59\t\n60\tMath Notation Standards:\n61\t- Greek letters: $\\alpha, \\beta, \\gamma, \\delta, \\lambda, \\mu, \\sigma, \\theta, \\phi, \\psi, \\omega$\n62\t- Operators: $\\sum, \\prod, \\int, \\partial, \\nabla, \\infty$\n63\t- Relations: $\\leq, \\geq, \\neq, \\approx, \\equiv, \\in, \\subset$\n64\t- Functions: $\\log, \\ln, \\sin, \\cos, \\exp, \\sqrt{}, \\frac{}{}$\n65\t- Define all symbols clearly on first use\n66\t\n67\tPROSE STYLE:\n68\t\n69\tProfessional/Academic Voice:\n70\t- Impersonal, objective tone\n71\t- Present tense for established facts, past tense for specific studies\n72\t- Avoid promotional/commercial language\n73\t- Remove instructional tone\n74\t- Convert to neutral academic phrasing\n75\t\n76\tProhibited rhetorical modes (applies to all workflows)\n77\t- Instructional language: no imperatives, reader directions, or authorial roadmaps (e.g., addressing what the reader should do or what the text \"will\" do next)\n78\t- Motivational/promotional claims: no value‑laden benefits to an audience, no capability or marketing assertions about the work; avoid audience‑targeted promises\n79\t- Second‑person address: do not address the reader directly\n80\t- Rewrite such expressions as descriptive, evidence‑based statements in an impersonal, declarative form\n81\t\n82\tProblematic Language Patterns (convert to academic tone):\n83\t- \"enables\" -> \"permits\", \"allows\", \"makes possible\"\n84\t- \"powerful\"-> \"effective\", \"capable\"\n85\t- \"cutting-edge\" → \"recent\", \"current\", \"modern\"\n86\t\n87\tCHARACTER FORMATTING:\n88\t- For ENGLISH outputs: ASCII text only (no accented letters or non-ASCII punctuation)\n89\t- For non-English outputs: allow the minimal extended Latin characters required by the target language (e.g., á, é, ñ) but keep punctuation ASCII\n90\t- Straight quotes (\", ') not smart quotes (\", \", ', ')\n91\t- Standard hyphens (-) not em-dashes (—)\n92\t- No special Unicode symbols outside LaTeX math\n93\t\n94\tDE-DUPLICATION AND CONSISTENCY CHECKS:\n95\t- Identify and remove duplicated sentences, sections, tables, or figures.\n96\t- Detect contradictory statements; reconcile using KB and external citations; if unresolved, keep the more reliable/cited version.\n97\t- Ensure units, symbols, and terminology are consistent throughout.\n98\t\n99\tCITATIONS — Session workflow policy (RESEARCH/REVIEW)\n100\t\n101\tMANDATORY REQUIREMENT:\n102\t- PRESERVE all token citations in the final document exactly as they appear\n103\t- Supported tokens: [KB:filename.md], [WEB:https://...], [DOI:...], [ARXIV:...]\n104\t- Do NOT convert tokens to [@key] format\n105\t- Do NOT remove any citation tokens\n106\t- Reasoning: Token citations are required for downstream audit verification and traceability\n107\t\n108\tDeduplication:\n109\t- Ensure a single consistent citation per claim\n110\t- If multiple tokens cite the same source, keep one representative token\n111\t- Place citations immediately after the sentence they support\n112\t\n113\tCODE BLOCKS (MARKDOWN/PANDOC/QUARTO)\n114\t- Treat fenced code blocks (backticks or tildes; length >= 3, including 4+; may include Quarto language headers like `{r}` on the opening fence) and indented code blocks as **opaque**.\n115\t- Do NOT modify content inside code blocks during formatting.\n116\t- Do NOT insert citations or audit footnotes inside code blocks; place any required metadata outside the block (typically immediately after the closing fence).\n117\t\n118\tFILESYSTEM AND LOG ACCESS CONSTRAINTS:\n119\t- NEVER read or use orchestration log files as input to any workflow. In particular, you MUST NOT read files such as `agent.out`, `RUN_METADATA.txt`, or other raw transcripts produced by the execution layer; they exist only for debugging on disk and must not be sent back into Claude-flow or used as evidence.\n120\t- When using filesystem tools (Read/Glob/Bash/find), skip any entries named exactly `current` or obvious symlinks to `*/sessions/run-*`; treat them as internal implementation details, not as documents or data sources.\n121\t\n122\tOUTPUT REQUIREMENT:\n123\tProduce a publication-ready professional document requiring no additional formatting.\n124\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7d52a3ef-833c-4d3d-a19f-380d6d5297be","uuid":"709746d5-2c9d-4707-b96b-0f5a64b7e3a1","timestamp":"2026-07-06T17:09:56.490Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/usr/local/libexec/tito/rules/format.session.md","content":"PROFESSIONAL DOCUMENT STYLE FORMATTING\n\nPurpose: Transform technical documents into publication-ready professional format.\n\nIMPORTANT: BEFORE FORMATTING - ANALYZE DOCUMENT STATE\n\nPRE-FORMAT ANALYSIS REQUIREMENTS:\n1. **Check Current Format**: Assess if the document already meets professional clarity and consistency\n2. **Mathematical Notation Check**: Verify if equations use proper LaTeX format ($...$, $$...$$) where applicable\n3. **Citation Format**: Ensure citations follow project token policy or normalized [@key]\n4. **Quality Assessment**: Determine if the document exhibits publication-ready professional characteristics\n5. **De-duplication and Consistency**: Detect duplicated content and potential contradictions; resolve when possible\n\nQUALITY TARGETS (NON-GATING):\n- Clear organization suitable for a professional technical audience\n- Mathematical expressions (if present) in LaTeX format ($...$ or `$$...$$`)\n- Consistent, normalized citations (project tokens or [@key])\n- Professional/academic voice and consistent technical terminology\n\nNote: These targets are mandatory goals to achieve. Always apply all formatting checks regardless of perceived document quality.\n\nMATHEMATICAL EXPRESSIONS:\n\nInline Math ($...$ format):\n- Use for mathematical symbols, variables, and simple expressions within text\n- Examples: $x$, $f(x)$, $n > 100$, $\\alpha = 0.05$, $O(n \\log n)$\n- Use for brief formulas: $E[X] = \\mu$, $\\beta_1 = \\mu_3^2/\\mu_2^3$\n- Single-line expressions that flow with text must be short: $Z = \\gamma + \\delta f((X - \\xi)/\\lambda)$\n\nDisplay Math ($$...$$ format):\n- Use for standalone equations that deserve emphasis or are complex\n- Multi-line expressions, definitions, theorems\n- Integration/summation with multiple terms: $$H[f] = -\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f(x) \\log f(x) dx$$\n- Matrix expressions, optimization problems\n- Equations with fractions, large operators, or multiple levels: $$\\kappa = \\frac{\\beta_1(\\beta_2+3)^2}{4(4\\beta_2-3\\beta_1)(2\\beta_2-3\\beta_1-6)}$$\n- Piecewise functions, case definitions\n\nDisplay Math Promotion Gate:\n- Before final save, scan every inline `$...$` math span.\n- Convert an inline span to a standalone `$$...$$` display block when the\n  span is a complete equation or definition and contains any of these\n  display triggers: `\\int`, `\\sum`, `\\prod`, `\\lim`, `\\max`, `\\min`,\n  `\\frac{`, `\\sqrt{`, `\\left`, `\\right`, `\\begin{`, `cases`, `matrix`,\n  `aligned`.\n- Convert named engineering index or ratio definitions to display math when\n  the equation is the object being defined, for example peak/RMS/effective\n  strain measures, CSR, MSF, LPI, LSN, FOS/FS, PGA-derived demand, or\n  pore-pressure ratio definitions.\n- Keep inline math for short symbols, units, variable references, and compact\n  relations that are incidental to the sentence.\n- Do not duplicate the equation: promote by moving the equation to display\n  form and keep the surrounding prose as prose.\n\nFORBIDDEN Math Formats:\n- Never use \\(...\\) or \\[...\\]\n- No Unicode symbols in equations (β, α, ∑, ∫)\n- No inconsistent notation (use $\\lambda$ throughout, not switching to $l$)\n- No equations in code blocks unless showing actual code\n\nMath Notation Standards:\n- Greek letters: $\\alpha, \\beta, \\gamma, \\delta, \\lambda, \\mu, \\sigma, \\theta, \\phi, \\psi, \\omega$\n- Operators: $\\sum, \\prod, \\int, \\partial, \\nabla, \\infty$\n- Relations: $\\leq, \\geq, \\neq, \\approx, \\equiv, \\in, \\subset$\n- Functions: $\\log, \\ln, \\sin, \\cos, \\exp, \\sqrt{}, \\frac{}{}$\n- Define all symbols clearly on first use\n\nPROSE STYLE:\n\nProfessional/Academic Voice:\n- Impersonal, objective tone\n- Present tense for established facts, past tense for specific studies\n- Avoid promotional/commercial language\n- Remove instructional tone\n- Convert to neutral academic phrasing\n\nProhibited rhetorical modes (applies to all workflows)\n- Instructional language: no imperatives, reader directions, or authorial roadmaps (e.g., addressing what the reader should do or what the text \"will\" do next)\n- Motivational/promotional claims: no value‑laden benefits to an audience, no capability or marketing assertions about the work; avoid audience‑targeted promises\n- Second‑person address: do not address the reader directly\n- Rewrite such expressions as descriptive, evidence‑based statements in an impersonal, declarative form\n\nProblematic Language Patterns (convert to academic tone):\n- \"enables\" -> \"permits\", \"allows\", \"makes possible\"\n- \"powerful\"-> \"effective\", \"capable\"\n- \"cutting-edge\" → \"recent\", \"current\", \"modern\"\n\nCHARACTER FORMATTING:\n- For ENGLISH outputs: ASCII text only (no accented letters or non-ASCII punctuation)\n- For non-English outputs: allow the minimal extended Latin characters required by the target language (e.g., á, é, ñ) but keep punctuation ASCII\n- Straight quotes (\", ') not smart quotes (\", \", ', ')\n- Standard hyphens (-) not em-dashes (—)\n- No special Unicode symbols outside LaTeX math\n\nDE-DUPLICATION AND CONSISTENCY CHECKS:\n- Identify and remove duplicated sentences, sections, tables, or figures.\n- Detect contradictory statements; reconcile using KB and external citations; if unresolved, keep the more reliable/cited version.\n- Ensure units, symbols, and terminology are consistent throughout.\n\nCITATIONS — Session workflow policy (RESEARCH/REVIEW)\n\nMANDATORY REQUIREMENT:\n- PRESERVE all token citations in the final document exactly as they appear\n- Supported tokens: [KB:filename.md], [WEB:https://...], [DOI:...], [ARXIV:...]\n- Do NOT convert tokens to [@key] format\n- Do NOT remove any citation tokens\n- Reasoning: Token citations are required for downstream audit verification and traceability\n\nDeduplication:\n- Ensure a single consistent citation per claim\n- If multiple tokens cite the same source, keep one representative token\n- Place citations immediately after the sentence they support\n\nCODE BLOCKS (MARKDOWN/PANDOC/QUARTO)\n- Treat fenced code blocks (backticks or tildes; length >= 3, including 4+; may include Quarto language headers like `{r}` on the opening fence) and indented code blocks as **opaque**.\n- Do NOT modify content inside code blocks during formatting.\n- Do NOT insert citations or audit footnotes inside code blocks; place any required metadata outside the block (typically immediately after the closing fence).\n\nFILESYSTEM AND LOG ACCESS CONSTRAINTS:\n- NEVER read or use orchestration log files as input to any workflow. In particular, you MUST NOT read files such as `agent.out`, `RUN_METADATA.txt`, or other raw transcripts produced by the execution layer; they exist only for debugging on disk and must not be sent back into Claude-flow or used as evidence.\n- When using filesystem tools (Read/Glob/Bash/find), skip any entries named exactly `current` or obvious symlinks to `*/sessions/run-*`; treat them as internal implementation details, not as documents or data sources.\n\nOUTPUT REQUIREMENT:\nProduce a publication-ready professional document requiring no additional formatting.\n","numLines":124,"startLine":1,"totalLines":124}}}
