{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Cbk3UottSygkmvn5i8U2ge","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"cumulative absolute velocity CAV liquefaction intensity measure ground motion\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"A simple ground‐motion prediction model for cumulative absolute velocity and model validation | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/263597010_A_simple_ground-motion_prediction_model_for_cumulative_absolute_velocity_and_model_validation\"},{\"title\":\"A simple ground‐motion prediction model for cumulative absolute velocity and model validation - Du - 2013 - Earthquake Engineering & Structural Dynamics - Wiley Online Library\",\"url\":\"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/eqe.2266\"},{\"title\":\"A cumulative absolute velocity prediction method based on surrounding strong motion records and deep learning - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0952197625014186\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) A Ground Motion Prediction Equation for the Horizontal Component of Cumulative Absolute Velocity (CAV) Based on the PEER-NGA Strong Motion Database\",\"url\":\"https://www.academia.edu/60448954/A_Ground_Motion_Prediction_Equation_for_the_Horizontal_Component_of_Cumulative_Absolute_Velocity_CAV_Based_on_the_PEER_NGA_Strong_Motion_Database\"},{\"title\":\"Ground-motion model for the standardized version of cumulative absolute velocity - Kenneth W Campbell, Yousef Bozorgnia, 2023\",\"url\":\"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/87552930221144063\"},{\"title\":\"Performance evaluation of machine learning techniques in predicting cumulative absolute velocity - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0267726123004207\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Ground Motion Models for the Horizontal Components of Arias Intensity (AI) and Cumulative Absolute Velocity (CAV) Using the NGA-West2 Database\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/334061329_Ground_Motion_Models_for_the_Horizontal_Components_of_Arias_Intensity_AI_and_Cumulative_Absolute_Velocity_CAV_Using_the_NGA-West2_Database\"},{\"title\":\"Empirical correlations between cumulative absolute velocity and spectral accelerations from NGA ground motion database - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0267726112001868\"},{\"title\":\"Probabilistic Liquefaction Triggering and Manifestation Models Based on Cumulative Absolute Velocity | Journal of Geotechnical and Geoenvironmental Engineering | Vol 148, No 3\",\"url\":\"https://ascelibrary.com/doi/10.1061/(ASCE)GT.1943-5606.0002729\"},{\"title\":\"Ground Motion Intensity Measures for Liquefaction Hazard Evaluation | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/238439703_Ground_Motion_Intensity_Measures_for_Liquefaction_Hazard_Evaluation\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's comprehensive information about cumulative absolute velocity (CAV) as a liquefaction intensity measure:\n\n## Definition and Mathematical Formulation\n\nCumulative Absolute Velocity (CAV) is mathematically defined as the cumulative integral of the absolute value of ground acceleration over time: CAV = ∫₀ᵀ |a(t)| dt, where a(t) is the time-varying ground acceleration, and T is the total duration of the ground motion time history.\n\n## Role as a Ground Motion Intensity Measure\n\nCumulative absolute velocity (CAV) is an important ground motion intensity measure used in seismic hazard analysis. CAV comprehensively accounts for both the amplitude and duration characteristics of ground motion, providing a reliable metric for evaluating the potential destructiveness of seismic events.\n\n## Application to Liquefaction\n\nCAV has been used as an index to indicate the possible onset of structural damage to nuclear power plant facilities and liquefaction of saturated soils. Energy-related IMs such as CAV5 have a superior predictive capacity than the peak ground acceleration in predicting the liquefaction initiation and permanent displacement.\n\nThe capacity cumulative absolute velocity (CAVc) has been proposed as a novel measure for the resistance of granular soils to earthquake-induced liquefaction.\n\n## Advantages as an Intensity Measure\n\nThe interevent, intra-event, and intracomponent standard deviations from CAV relationships are smaller than any intensity measure investigated thus far. This low variability makes CAV an attractive option for earthquake engineering applications.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7d52a3ef-833c-4d3d-a19f-380d6d5297be","uuid":"360cc95a-d757-49e8-ba7a-9cbff00b5b86","timestamp":"2026-07-06T17:10:54.596Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"cumulative absolute velocity CAV liquefaction intensity measure ground motion","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_019NV6aERcKFNUhR8cuBYSTE","content":[{"title":"A simple ground‐motion prediction model for cumulative absolute velocity and model validation | Request PDF","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/263597010_A_simple_ground-motion_prediction_model_for_cumulative_absolute_velocity_and_model_validation"},{"title":"A simple ground‐motion prediction model for cumulative absolute velocity and model validation - Du - 2013 - Earthquake Engineering & Structural Dynamics - Wiley Online Library","url":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/eqe.2266"},{"title":"A cumulative absolute velocity prediction method based on surrounding strong motion records and deep learning - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0952197625014186"},{"title":"(PDF) A Ground Motion Prediction Equation for the Horizontal Component of Cumulative Absolute Velocity (CAV) Based on the PEER-NGA Strong Motion Database","url":"https://www.academia.edu/60448954/A_Ground_Motion_Prediction_Equation_for_the_Horizontal_Component_of_Cumulative_Absolute_Velocity_CAV_Based_on_the_PEER_NGA_Strong_Motion_Database"},{"title":"Ground-motion model for the standardized version of cumulative absolute velocity - Kenneth W Campbell, Yousef Bozorgnia, 2023","url":"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/87552930221144063"},{"title":"Performance evaluation of machine learning techniques in predicting cumulative absolute velocity - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0267726123004207"},{"title":"(PDF) Ground Motion Models for the Horizontal Components of Arias Intensity (AI) and Cumulative Absolute Velocity (CAV) Using the NGA-West2 Database","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/334061329_Ground_Motion_Models_for_the_Horizontal_Components_of_Arias_Intensity_AI_and_Cumulative_Absolute_Velocity_CAV_Using_the_NGA-West2_Database"},{"title":"Empirical correlations between cumulative absolute velocity and spectral accelerations from NGA ground motion database - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0267726112001868"},{"title":"Probabilistic Liquefaction Triggering and Manifestation Models Based on Cumulative Absolute Velocity | Journal of Geotechnical and Geoenvironmental Engineering | Vol 148, No 3","url":"https://ascelibrary.com/doi/10.1061/(ASCE)GT.1943-5606.0002729"},{"title":"Ground Motion Intensity Measures for Liquefaction Hazard Evaluation | Request PDF","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/238439703_Ground_Motion_Intensity_Measures_for_Liquefaction_Hazard_Evaluation"}]},"Based on the search results, here's comprehensive information about cumulative absolute velocity (CAV) as a liquefaction intensity measure:\n\n## Definition and Mathematical Formulation\n\nCumulative Absolute Velocity (CAV) is mathematically defined as the cumulative integral of the absolute value of ground acceleration over time: CAV = ∫₀ᵀ |a(t)| dt, where a(t) is the time-varying ground acceleration, and T is the total duration of the ground motion time history.\n\n## Role as a Ground Motion Intensity Measure\n\nCumulative absolute velocity (CAV) is an important ground motion intensity measure used in seismic hazard analysis. CAV comprehensively accounts for both the amplitude and duration characteristics of ground motion, providing a reliable metric for evaluating the potential destructiveness of seismic events.\n\n## Application to Liquefaction\n\nCAV has been used as an index to indicate the possible onset of structural damage to nuclear power plant facilities and liquefaction of saturated soils. Energy-related IMs such as CAV5 have a superior predictive capacity than the peak ground acceleration in predicting the liquefaction initiation and permanent displacement.\n\nThe capacity cumulative absolute velocity (CAVc) has been proposed as a novel measure for the resistance of granular soils to earthquake-induced liquefaction.\n\n## Advantages as an Intensity Measure\n\nThe interevent, intra-event, and intracomponent standard deviations from CAV relationships are smaller than any intensity measure investigated thus far. This low variability makes CAV an attractive option for earthquake engineering applications."],"durationSeconds":8.341966166999999,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ggd888CfdMZsKEts6jaWSY","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Arias intensity pore pressure generation liquefaction geotechnical engineering\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Energy-Based Methods for Seismic Soil Liquefaction: Past, Present, and Future | Indian Geotechnical Journal | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40098-026-01480-y\"},{\"title\":\"A New Method for Evaluating Liquefaction by Energy-Based Pore Water Pressure Models\",\"url\":\"https://www.mdpi.com/2079-6412/15/1/7\"},{\"title\":\"A-New-Method-for-Evaluating-Liquefaction-by-Energy-Based- ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/journal/Coatings-2079-6412/publication/387374977_A_New_Method_for_Evaluating_Liquefaction_by_Energy-Based_Pore_Water_Pressure_Models/links/68037ff960241d51400df2ca/A-New-Method-for-Evaluating-Liquefaction-by-Energy-Based-Pore-Water-Pressure-Models.pdf?origin=scientificContributions\"},{\"title\":\"Effects of seismic input, fine crust and existing structure on liquefaction from centrifuge model tests | Bulletin of Earthquake Engineering\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10518-021-01139-4\"},{\"title\":\"Energy-Based Liquefaction Triggering Model | Journal of Geotechnical and Geoenvironmental Engineering | Vol 149, No 11\",\"url\":\"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/10.1061/JGGEFK.GTENG-11402\"},{\"title\":\"An energy-based pore water pressure model for evaluating liquefaction of saturated sands | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/386134411_An_energy-based_pore_water_pressure_model_for_evaluating_liquefaction_of_saturated_sands\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Assessment of Liquefaction Potential during Earthquakes by Arias Intensity\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/273026153_Assessment_of_Liquefaction_Potential_during_Earthquakes_by_Arias_Intensity\"},{\"title\":\"Proposal of liquefaction potential assessment procedure using real earthquake loading | KSCE Journal of Civil Engineering | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12205-008-8015-9\"},{\"title\":\"A high-fidelity seismic intensity measure to assess dynamic liquefaction   in tailings\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2204.11393\"},{\"title\":\"Transient Loading Effects on Pore Pressure Generation and the Response of Liquefiable Soils | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-11898-2_5\"}]\n\nI've completed the web search for your query about Arias intensity, pore pressure generation, liquefaction, and geotechnical engineering. Here are the key findings from the search results:\n\n## Key Findings\n\n**Energy-Based Assessment Methods:**\nThe energy of the earthquake is quantified using the Arias intensity, and an increase in Arias intensity correlates with a higher energy level of ground motion, which subsequently reduces the cyclic stress ratio necessary for soil liquefaction.\n\n**Pore Pressure Generation and Liquefaction Timing:**\nLiquefaction timing estimates based on Arias Intensity and SER can recalibrate soil models for G/Gmax, energy absorption, and pore pressure rise. Additionally, incorporating a ramp that accounts for pore water pressure buildup in the trilinear function improved liquefaction triggering estimation.\n\n**Thresholds and Field Applications:**\nThe Arias intensity intercept at low penetration values is 0.22 m/s for both SPT and CPT field evaluation methods, representing the lowest Arias Intensity likely to trigger initial seismic soil liquefaction potential in the field for the lowest capacity soils.\n\n**Practical Implications:**\nStudies have highlighted a correlation between liquefaction-induced building settlement and intensity measures linked to Arias intensity, suggesting the possibility of using alternative earthquake parameters than PGA to evaluate the consequence of liquefaction, and centrifuge results have confirmed a dependence of superficial settlement on Arias intensity.\n\nThe search results reveal that energy-based methods using Arias intensity represent an emerging alternative to traditional stress-based approaches for evaluating liquefaction potential in geotechnical engineering.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7d52a3ef-833c-4d3d-a19f-380d6d5297be","uuid":"7364aea9-bf15-4380-9f0d-3dd92a05e969","timestamp":"2026-07-06T17:10:55.043Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"Arias intensity pore pressure generation liquefaction geotechnical engineering","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01FR5gkVVGX7QBRv8ocJKeE6","content":[{"title":"Energy-Based Methods for Seismic Soil Liquefaction: Past, Present, and Future | Indian Geotechnical Journal | Springer Nature Link","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40098-026-01480-y"},{"title":"A New Method for Evaluating Liquefaction by Energy-Based Pore Water Pressure Models","url":"https://www.mdpi.com/2079-6412/15/1/7"},{"title":"A-New-Method-for-Evaluating-Liquefaction-by-Energy-Based- ...","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/journal/Coatings-2079-6412/publication/387374977_A_New_Method_for_Evaluating_Liquefaction_by_Energy-Based_Pore_Water_Pressure_Models/links/68037ff960241d51400df2ca/A-New-Method-for-Evaluating-Liquefaction-by-Energy-Based-Pore-Water-Pressure-Models.pdf?origin=scientificContributions"},{"title":"Effects of seismic input, fine crust and existing structure on liquefaction from centrifuge model tests | Bulletin of Earthquake Engineering","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10518-021-01139-4"},{"title":"Energy-Based Liquefaction Triggering Model | Journal of Geotechnical and Geoenvironmental Engineering | Vol 149, No 11","url":"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/10.1061/JGGEFK.GTENG-11402"},{"title":"An energy-based pore water pressure model for evaluating liquefaction of saturated sands | Request PDF","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/386134411_An_energy-based_pore_water_pressure_model_for_evaluating_liquefaction_of_saturated_sands"},{"title":"(PDF) Assessment of Liquefaction Potential during Earthquakes by Arias Intensity","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/273026153_Assessment_of_Liquefaction_Potential_during_Earthquakes_by_Arias_Intensity"},{"title":"Proposal of liquefaction potential assessment procedure using real earthquake loading | KSCE Journal of Civil Engineering | Springer Nature Link","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12205-008-8015-9"},{"title":"A high-fidelity seismic intensity measure to assess dynamic liquefaction   in tailings","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2204.11393"},{"title":"Transient Loading Effects on Pore Pressure Generation and the Response of Liquefiable Soils | Springer Nature Link","url":"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-11898-2_5"}]},"I've completed the web search for your query about Arias intensity, pore pressure generation, liquefaction, and geotechnical engineering. Here are the key findings from the search results:\n\n## Key Findings\n\n**Energy-Based Assessment Methods:**\nThe energy of the earthquake is quantified using the Arias intensity, and an increase in Arias intensity correlates with a higher energy level of ground motion, which subsequently reduces the cyclic stress ratio necessary for soil liquefaction.\n\n**Pore Pressure Generation and Liquefaction Timing:**\nLiquefaction timing estimates based on Arias Intensity and SER can recalibrate soil models for G/Gmax, energy absorption, and pore pressure rise. Additionally, incorporating a ramp that accounts for pore water pressure buildup in the trilinear function improved liquefaction triggering estimation.\n\n**Thresholds and Field Applications:**\nThe Arias intensity intercept at low penetration values is 0.22 m/s for both SPT and CPT field evaluation methods, representing the lowest Arias Intensity likely to trigger initial seismic soil liquefaction potential in the field for the lowest capacity soils.\n\n**Practical Implications:**\nStudies have highlighted a correlation between liquefaction-induced building settlement and intensity measures linked to Arias intensity, suggesting the possibility of using alternative earthquake parameters than PGA to evaluate the consequence of liquefaction, and centrifuge results have confirmed a dependence of superficial settlement on Arias intensity.\n\nThe search results reveal that energy-based methods using Arias intensity represent an emerging alternative to traditional stress-based approaches for evaluating liquefaction potential in geotechnical engineering."],"durationSeconds":9.133958375000002,"searchCount":1}}
