{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HHrwon6jhSzbvXYJGZEoyg","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"significant duration D5-95 liquefaction pore pressure accumulation blast vibration\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Pore Pressure Measurement in Blast-Induced Liquefaction Experiments | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/245561710_Pore_Pressure_Measurement_in_Blast-Induced_Liquefaction_Experiments\"},{\"title\":\"Canadian Liquefaction Experiment (CANLEX): Blast-Induced Ground Motion and Pore Pressure Experiments\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/228670098_Canadian_Liquefaction_Experiment_CANLEX_Blast-Induced_Ground_Motion_and_Pore_Pressure_Experiments\"},{\"title\":\"AD-A171 137 BLAST INDUCED LIQUEFACTION POTENTIAL AND TRANSIENT i/3\",\"url\":\"https://apps.dtic.mil/sti/tr/pdf/ADA173137.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Blast-Induced Pore Pressure and Liquefaction of Saturated Sand\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/277684217_Blast-Induced_Pore_Pressure_and_Liquefaction_of_Saturated_Sand\"},{\"title\":\"A New Method for Evaluating Liquefaction by Energy-Based Pore Water Pressure Models\",\"url\":\"https://www.mdpi.com/2079-6412/15/1/7\"},{\"title\":\"Blast-Induced Pore Pressure and Liquefaction of Saturated Sand | Journal of Geotechnical and Geoenvironmental Engineering | Vol 139, No 8\",\"url\":\"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/abs/10.1061/(ASCE)GT.1943-5606.0000846\"},{\"title\":\"Evaluating changes in shear wave velocity due to blast-induced liquefaction - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0267726123005250\"},{\"title\":\"Pore Pressure Measurement in Blast-Induced Liquefaction Experiments - Kyle M. Rollins, J. Dusty Lane, Emily Dibb, Scott A. Ashford, A. Gray Mullins, 2005\",\"url\":\"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/0361198105193600124\"}]\n\nThe search results provide information on blast-induced liquefaction and pore pressure accumulation. Here's what I found:\n\n## Key Findings:\n\nBlast-induced liquefaction experiments have been conducted at a number of test sites to evaluate lateral foundation resistance and soil improvement techniques.\n\nField tests using explosive generated spherical stress waves have been used to induce residual excess pore pressure and liquefaction in large saturated sand specimens.\n\nSite-specific empirical models for blast-induced vibration parameters like peak particle velocity (PPV), peak pressure (PP), peak particle displacement (PPD), and pore pressure ratio (PPR) are commonly used for blast-resistant designs.\n\nTesting was completed quickly following blasting to evaluate changes in soil stiffness due to excess pore pressures.\n\n## Blast Duration Parameters:\n\nWhile the search results discuss pore pressure accumulation and liquefaction from blast vibrations, they don't specifically address the \"D5-95\" parameter (which typically refers to the duration from 5% to 95% of maximum acceleration in seismic/vibration analysis) in relation to liquefaction. The results focus more on peak particle velocity, peak pressure, and peak particle displacement as key parameters for evaluating liquefaction potential.\n\nIf you need more specific information about D5-95 significant duration metrics in blast vibration analysis, please let me know and I can conduct an additional search.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7d52a3ef-833c-4d3d-a19f-380d6d5297be","uuid":"26b1ccec-2fdd-4c4a-9aca-b07cf6f099e6","timestamp":"2026-07-06T17:11:09.123Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"significant duration D5-95 liquefaction pore pressure accumulation blast vibration","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01LDYoY1XQhKeXMUbwvdbxHN","content":[{"title":"Pore Pressure Measurement in Blast-Induced Liquefaction Experiments | Request PDF","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/245561710_Pore_Pressure_Measurement_in_Blast-Induced_Liquefaction_Experiments"},{"title":"Canadian Liquefaction Experiment (CANLEX): Blast-Induced Ground Motion and Pore Pressure Experiments","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/228670098_Canadian_Liquefaction_Experiment_CANLEX_Blast-Induced_Ground_Motion_and_Pore_Pressure_Experiments"},{"title":"AD-A171 137 BLAST INDUCED LIQUEFACTION POTENTIAL AND TRANSIENT i/3","url":"https://apps.dtic.mil/sti/tr/pdf/ADA173137.pdf"},{"title":"Blast-Induced Pore Pressure and Liquefaction of Saturated Sand","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/277684217_Blast-Induced_Pore_Pressure_and_Liquefaction_of_Saturated_Sand"},{"title":"A New Method for Evaluating Liquefaction by Energy-Based Pore Water Pressure Models","url":"https://www.mdpi.com/2079-6412/15/1/7"},{"title":"Blast-Induced Pore Pressure and Liquefaction of Saturated Sand | Journal of Geotechnical and Geoenvironmental Engineering | Vol 139, No 8","url":"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/abs/10.1061/(ASCE)GT.1943-5606.0000846"},{"title":"Evaluating changes in shear wave velocity due to blast-induced liquefaction - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0267726123005250"},{"title":"Pore Pressure Measurement in Blast-Induced Liquefaction Experiments - Kyle M. Rollins, J. Dusty Lane, Emily Dibb, Scott A. Ashford, A. Gray Mullins, 2005","url":"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/0361198105193600124"}]},"The search results provide information on blast-induced liquefaction and pore pressure accumulation. Here's what I found:\n\n## Key Findings:\n\nBlast-induced liquefaction experiments have been conducted at a number of test sites to evaluate lateral foundation resistance and soil improvement techniques.\n\nField tests using explosive generated spherical stress waves have been used to induce residual excess pore pressure and liquefaction in large saturated sand specimens.\n\nSite-specific empirical models for blast-induced vibration parameters like peak particle velocity (PPV), peak pressure (PP), peak particle displacement (PPD), and pore pressure ratio (PPR) are commonly used for blast-resistant designs.\n\nTesting was completed quickly following blasting to evaluate changes in soil stiffness due to excess pore pressures.\n\n## Blast Duration Parameters:\n\nWhile the search results discuss pore pressure accumulation and liquefaction from blast vibrations, they don't specifically address the \"D5-95\" parameter (which typically refers to the duration from 5% to 95% of maximum acceleration in seismic/vibration analysis) in relation to liquefaction. The results focus more on peak particle velocity, peak pressure, and peak particle displacement as key parameters for evaluating liquefaction potential.\n\nIf you need more specific information about D5-95 significant duration metrics in blast vibration analysis, please let me know and I can conduct an additional search."],"durationSeconds":9.883038624999987,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016LfxKVwM9XKpVdnBEjhwrv","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Seed Idriss cyclic stress ratio equivalent uniform cycles liquefaction triggering\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Stress-Based CPTu Cyclic Liquefaction Triggering Procedures: Improvements from a State Parameter Approach | Journal of Geotechnical and Geoenvironmental Engineering | Vol 151, No 4\",\"url\":\"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/10.1061/JGGEFK.GTENG-12857\"},{\"title\":\"Probability-based assessment of number of equivalent uniform stress cycles - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0267726121000051\"},{\"title\":\"Assessment of seismic soil liquefaction triggering beneath building foundation systems - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0267726112001789\"},{\"title\":\"© 2025 Rocscience Inc. Settle3 Liquefaction Theory Manual\",\"url\":\"https://static.rocscience.cloud/assets/verification-and-theory/Settle3/Settle3-Liquefaction-Theory-Manual.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"On the determination of cyclic shear stress for soil liquefaction triggering in centrifuge model test - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0267726123003822\"},{\"title\":\"CPT AND SPT BASED LIQUEFACTION TRIGGERING ...\",\"url\":\"http://www.ce.memphis.edu/7137/PDFs/Notes/i3Boulanger_Idriss_CPT_and_SPT_Liq_triggering_CGM-14-01_20141.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"SPT-BASED LIQUEFACTION TRIGGERING PROCEDURES\",\"url\":\"https://faculty.engineering.ucdavis.edu/boulanger/wp-content/uploads/sites/71/2014/09/Idriss_Boulanger_SPT_Liquefaction_CGM-10-02.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"4 The Simplified Stress-Based Approach to Triggering ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.nationalacademies.org/read/23474/chapter/6\"},{\"title\":\"Liquefaction Potential Interactive Calculator | FIRGELLI\",\"url\":\"https://www.firgelliauto.com/blogs/calculators/liquefaction-potential-crr-csr-calculator\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about Seed-Idriss cyclic stress ratio, equivalent uniform cycles, and liquefaction triggering:\n\n## Overview\n\nThe simplified stress-based procedure (Seed and Idriss, 1971) is the one most commonly used in practice to predict liquefaction triggering. A factor of safety (FS) against liquefaction triggering is computed as the ratio between the seismic loading required to trigger liquefaction (i.e., the liquefaction resistance) and the seismic loading expected from the earthquake (i.e., the seismic demand).\n\n## Equivalent Uniform Cycles\n\nSeed and Idriss suggested to \"idealize the shear stress history at the various depths to determine the significant number of stress cycles, N, and the equivalent uniform cyclic shear stress developed at each level\". The reference earthquake magnitude in the original simplified procedure was Mw = 7.5, corresponding to approximately 15 equivalent uniform stress cycles.\n\n## Cyclic Stress Ratio Calculation\n\nThe choice of the reference stress level (i.e., the factor 0.65) was selected by Seed and Idriss (1967) and has been in use since. The coefficient 0.65 in the CSR equation represents the ratio of average cyclic shear stress to peak cyclic shear stress, derived from equivalent uniform loading concepts. This simplification allows complex irregular seismic loading to be represented by an equivalent uniform sinusoidal series of stress cycles.\n\n## Magnitude Scaling Factor\n\nMSFs were first developed by Seed and Idriss (1982) from estimates of the typical number of cycles of loading for different earthquake magnitudes and laboratory test results.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7d52a3ef-833c-4d3d-a19f-380d6d5297be","uuid":"33c3b783-47eb-475c-ada8-7b73b2fb76fc","timestamp":"2026-07-06T17:11:09.894Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"Seed Idriss cyclic stress ratio equivalent uniform cycles liquefaction triggering","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01MYJSjopfVhJvfWnWJUniVe","content":[{"title":"Stress-Based CPTu Cyclic Liquefaction Triggering Procedures: Improvements from a State Parameter Approach | Journal of Geotechnical and Geoenvironmental Engineering | Vol 151, No 4","url":"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/10.1061/JGGEFK.GTENG-12857"},{"title":"Probability-based assessment of number of equivalent uniform stress cycles - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0267726121000051"},{"title":"Assessment of seismic soil liquefaction triggering beneath building foundation systems - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0267726112001789"},{"title":"© 2025 Rocscience Inc. Settle3 Liquefaction Theory Manual","url":"https://static.rocscience.cloud/assets/verification-and-theory/Settle3/Settle3-Liquefaction-Theory-Manual.pdf"},{"title":"On the determination of cyclic shear stress for soil liquefaction triggering in centrifuge model test - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0267726123003822"},{"title":"CPT AND SPT BASED LIQUEFACTION TRIGGERING ...","url":"http://www.ce.memphis.edu/7137/PDFs/Notes/i3Boulanger_Idriss_CPT_and_SPT_Liq_triggering_CGM-14-01_20141.pdf"},{"title":"SPT-BASED LIQUEFACTION TRIGGERING PROCEDURES","url":"https://faculty.engineering.ucdavis.edu/boulanger/wp-content/uploads/sites/71/2014/09/Idriss_Boulanger_SPT_Liquefaction_CGM-10-02.pdf"},{"title":"4 The Simplified Stress-Based Approach to Triggering ...","url":"https://www.nationalacademies.org/read/23474/chapter/6"},{"title":"Liquefaction Potential Interactive Calculator | FIRGELLI","url":"https://www.firgelliauto.com/blogs/calculators/liquefaction-potential-crr-csr-calculator"}]},"Based on the search results, here's what I found about Seed-Idriss cyclic stress ratio, equivalent uniform cycles, and liquefaction triggering:\n\n## Overview\n\nThe simplified stress-based procedure (Seed and Idriss, 1971) is the one most commonly used in practice to predict liquefaction triggering. A factor of safety (FS) against liquefaction triggering is computed as the ratio between the seismic loading required to trigger liquefaction (i.e., the liquefaction resistance) and the seismic loading expected from the earthquake (i.e., the seismic demand).\n\n## Equivalent Uniform Cycles\n\nSeed and Idriss suggested to \"idealize the shear stress history at the various depths to determine the significant number of stress cycles, N, and the equivalent uniform cyclic shear stress developed at each level\". The reference earthquake magnitude in the original simplified procedure was Mw = 7.5, corresponding to approximately 15 equivalent uniform stress cycles.\n\n## Cyclic Stress Ratio Calculation\n\nThe choice of the reference stress level (i.e., the factor 0.65) was selected by Seed and Idriss (1967) and has been in use since. The coefficient 0.65 in the CSR equation represents the ratio of average cyclic shear stress to peak cyclic shear stress, derived from equivalent uniform loading concepts. This simplification allows complex irregular seismic loading to be represented by an equivalent uniform sinusoidal series of stress cycles.\n\n## Magnitude Scaling Factor\n\nMSFs were first developed by Seed and Idriss (1982) from estimates of the typical number of cycles of loading for different earthquake magnitudes and laboratory test results."],"durationSeconds":8.836997416999992,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NqwqzC12fjxQEKv15dZmyw","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"peak particle velocity PPV liquefaction pore pressure correlation limitations\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Blast-Induced Pore Pressure and Liquefaction of Saturated Sand\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/277684217_Blast-Induced_Pore_Pressure_and_Liquefaction_of_Saturated_Sand\"},{\"title\":\"Peak particle velocity (ppv max ) versus momentumdistance-correlation... | Download Scientific Diagram\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Peak-particle-velocity-ppv-max-versus-momentumdistance-correlation-plot-at-rhyolite_fig2_249657181\"},{\"title\":\"Blast-Induced Pore Pressure and Liquefaction of Saturated Sand | Journal of Geotechnical and Geoenvironmental Engineering | Vol 139, No 8\",\"url\":\"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/abs/10.1061/(ASCE)GT.1943-5606.0000846\"},{\"title\":\"Liquefaction susceptibility assessment using shear wave velocity: a review with a case study | Discover Geoscience | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s44288-025-00288-2\"},{\"title\":\"Correlations between pore water pressure development in sandy soil and CAV-based ground motion intensity measures - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0013795222002708\"},{\"title\":\"Prediction of peak particle velocity using hybrid random forest approach\",\"url\":\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC11680976/\"},{\"title\":\"Porewater pressure increases in soil and rock from underground chemical and nuclear explosions - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0013795296000324\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found regarding peak particle velocity (PPV), liquefaction, pore pressure correlation, and their limitations:\n\n## PPV-Liquefaction-Pore Pressure Relationship\n\nPeak radial particle velocity and peak strain required to induce a given pore pressure ratio increase with increasing relative density and effective stress. Research has established threshold velocities: Little or no residual pore pressure was induced at peak radial particle velocity less than approximately a certain threshold (with peak shear strain less than approximately 0.005% at peak stress), while liquefaction was approached when peak radial particle velocities exceeded certain thresholds in loose, dense, and very-dense specimens, respectively.\n\n## Key Limitations\n\nOne significant limitation is that site-specific empirical relationships for calculation of PPV are commonly used for estimation of blast loads in design, however, these relationships are not able to consider the variation in rock parameters and uncertainty of in situ conditions.\n\nAdditionally, when considering shear wave velocity testing approaches, measurements capture strains as small as 0.001%, whereas liquefaction and excess pore water pressure buildup occurred at medium to large strains (>0.1%). This indicates a strain-level mismatch between measurement capabilities and the actual conditions under which liquefaction occurs.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7d52a3ef-833c-4d3d-a19f-380d6d5297be","uuid":"c8ff477e-4da3-4aa4-acbc-598ea04597b9","timestamp":"2026-07-06T17:11:10.173Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"peak particle velocity PPV liquefaction pore pressure correlation limitations","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_011kPBLnAF4oKb9brPVXpqHD","content":[{"title":"Blast-Induced Pore Pressure and Liquefaction of Saturated Sand","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/277684217_Blast-Induced_Pore_Pressure_and_Liquefaction_of_Saturated_Sand"},{"title":"Peak particle velocity (ppv max ) versus momentumdistance-correlation... | Download Scientific Diagram","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Peak-particle-velocity-ppv-max-versus-momentumdistance-correlation-plot-at-rhyolite_fig2_249657181"},{"title":"Blast-Induced Pore Pressure and Liquefaction of Saturated Sand | Journal of Geotechnical and Geoenvironmental Engineering | Vol 139, No 8","url":"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/abs/10.1061/(ASCE)GT.1943-5606.0000846"},{"title":"Liquefaction susceptibility assessment using shear wave velocity: a review with a case study | Discover Geoscience | Springer Nature Link","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s44288-025-00288-2"},{"title":"Correlations between pore water pressure development in sandy soil and CAV-based ground motion intensity measures - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0013795222002708"},{"title":"Prediction of peak particle velocity using hybrid random forest approach","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC11680976/"},{"title":"Porewater pressure increases in soil and rock from underground chemical and nuclear explosions - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0013795296000324"}]},"Based on the search results, here's what I found regarding peak particle velocity (PPV), liquefaction, pore pressure correlation, and their limitations:\n\n## PPV-Liquefaction-Pore Pressure Relationship\n\nPeak radial particle velocity and peak strain required to induce a given pore pressure ratio increase with increasing relative density and effective stress. Research has established threshold velocities: Little or no residual pore pressure was induced at peak radial particle velocity less than approximately a certain threshold (with peak shear strain less than approximately 0.005% at peak stress), while liquefaction was approached when peak radial particle velocities exceeded certain thresholds in loose, dense, and very-dense specimens, respectively.\n\n## Key Limitations\n\nOne significant limitation is that site-specific empirical relationships for calculation of PPV are commonly used for estimation of blast loads in design, however, these relationships are not able to consider the variation in rock parameters and uncertainty of in situ conditions.\n\nAdditionally, when considering shear wave velocity testing approaches, measurements capture strains as small as 0.001%, whereas liquefaction and excess pore water pressure buildup occurred at medium to large strains (>0.1%). This indicates a strain-level mismatch between measurement capabilities and the actual conditions under which liquefaction occurs."],"durationSeconds":8.698233999999998,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Y4VfsZuvPgMw2RqPSmhXqq","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"blast induced pore pressure fine tailings frequency cycles cyclic loading\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Blast-Induced Pore Pressure and Liquefaction of Saturated Sand\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/277684217_Blast-Induced_Pore_Pressure_and_Liquefaction_of_Saturated_Sand\"},{\"title\":\"Intense vibration affects the pore water pressure in a tailing dam, with the tendency to induce dam liquefaction. In this study, experiments were performed wherein model tailing dams were completely liquefied by sustained horizontal dynamic loading to determine the effects of the vibration frequency, vibration amplitude, and tailing density on the pore water pressure. The results revealed four stages in the increase of the tailing pore water pressure under dynamic loading, namely, a slow increase, a rapid i\",\"url\":\"https://www.hindawi.com/journals/sv/2018/1921057/\"},{\"title\":\"Excess pore water pressure generation in fine granular materials under undrained cyclic triaxial loading | International Journal of Geo-Engineering | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s40703-023-00185-y\"},{\"title\":\"Canadian Liquefaction Experiment (CANLEX): Blast-Induced Ground Motion and Pore Pressure Experiments\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/228670098_Canadian_Liquefaction_Experiment_CANLEX_Blast-Induced_Ground_Motion_and_Pore_Pressure_Experiments\"},{\"title\":\"Pore Pressure Measurement in Blast-Induced Liquefaction Experiments | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/245561710_Pore_Pressure_Measurement_in_Blast-Induced_Liquefaction_Experiments\"},{\"title\":\"Variation of Pore Water Pressure in Tailing Sand under Dynamic Loading - Jin - 2018 - Shock and Vibration - Wiley Online Library\",\"url\":\"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1155/2018/1921057\"},{\"title\":\"Pore Pressure Measurement in Blast-Induced Liquefaction Experiments - Kyle M. Rollins, J. Dusty Lane, Emily Dibb, Scott A. Ashford, A. Gray Mullins, 2005\",\"url\":\"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/0361198105193600124\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Pore Pressure Generation Models for Sands and Silty Soils Subjected to Cyclic Loading\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/245294607_Pore_Pressure_Generation_Models_for_Sands_and_Silty_Soils_Subjected_to_Cyclic_Loading\"}]\n\nI've found relevant research on your topic. Here's what the search results reveal about blast-induced pore pressure in fine tailings under cyclic loading:\n\n## Key Findings:\n\n**Pore Water Pressure Response Under Dynamic Loading:**\n\nResearch on tailings shows that pore water pressure under dynamic loading progresses through four stages: a slow increase, a rapid increase, inducement of structural failure, and inducement of complete liquefaction.\n\n**Effects of Frequency and Loading Parameters:**\n\nLower frequency and smaller amplitude of vibration were found to increase the time required to achieve a given pore water pressure in dense tailings. The tailing liquefaction time varied nonlinearly with the height from the base of the tailing dam, with an initial decrease followed by an increase, depending on vibration frequency and amplitude and tailing density.\n\n**Fine Granular Materials and Cyclic Loading:**\n\nUndrained cyclic triaxial tests have been performed to study excess pore water pressure generation in fine granular materials including tailings, with evaluation based on density conditions, number of cycles, and applied cyclic stress ratios. Excess pore water pressure accumulated over time due to cyclic loading, with accumulation significantly dependent on densities, cyclic stress ratio values, and material types.\n\n**Factors Influencing Liquefaction:**\n\nThe magnitude of cyclic stress or strain affects liquefaction risk—the larger the cyclic stress or strain the fewer the number of cycles required to induce liquefaction.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7d52a3ef-833c-4d3d-a19f-380d6d5297be","uuid":"6a736ffa-ad60-499d-a0e0-d87a85668ede","timestamp":"2026-07-06T17:11:10.363Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"blast induced pore pressure fine tailings frequency cycles cyclic loading","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01K1bCx3nByC3RZUtXBkDKUB","content":[{"title":"Blast-Induced Pore Pressure and Liquefaction of Saturated Sand","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/277684217_Blast-Induced_Pore_Pressure_and_Liquefaction_of_Saturated_Sand"},{"title":"Intense vibration affects the pore water pressure in a tailing dam, with the tendency to induce dam liquefaction. In this study, experiments were performed wherein model tailing dams were completely liquefied by sustained horizontal dynamic loading to determine the effects of the vibration frequency, vibration amplitude, and tailing density on the pore water pressure. The results revealed four stages in the increase of the tailing pore water pressure under dynamic loading, namely, a slow increase, a rapid i","url":"https://www.hindawi.com/journals/sv/2018/1921057/"},{"title":"Excess pore water pressure generation in fine granular materials under undrained cyclic triaxial loading | International Journal of Geo-Engineering | Springer Nature Link","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s40703-023-00185-y"},{"title":"Canadian Liquefaction Experiment (CANLEX): Blast-Induced Ground Motion and Pore Pressure Experiments","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/228670098_Canadian_Liquefaction_Experiment_CANLEX_Blast-Induced_Ground_Motion_and_Pore_Pressure_Experiments"},{"title":"Pore Pressure Measurement in Blast-Induced Liquefaction Experiments | Request PDF","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/245561710_Pore_Pressure_Measurement_in_Blast-Induced_Liquefaction_Experiments"},{"title":"Variation of Pore Water Pressure in Tailing Sand under Dynamic Loading - Jin - 2018 - Shock and Vibration - Wiley Online Library","url":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1155/2018/1921057"},{"title":"Pore Pressure Measurement in Blast-Induced Liquefaction Experiments - Kyle M. Rollins, J. Dusty Lane, Emily Dibb, Scott A. Ashford, A. Gray Mullins, 2005","url":"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/0361198105193600124"},{"title":"(PDF) Pore Pressure Generation Models for Sands and Silty Soils Subjected to Cyclic Loading","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/245294607_Pore_Pressure_Generation_Models_for_Sands_and_Silty_Soils_Subjected_to_Cyclic_Loading"}]},"I've found relevant research on your topic. Here's what the search results reveal about blast-induced pore pressure in fine tailings under cyclic loading:\n\n## Key Findings:\n\n**Pore Water Pressure Response Under Dynamic Loading:**\n\nResearch on tailings shows that pore water pressure under dynamic loading progresses through four stages: a slow increase, a rapid increase, inducement of structural failure, and inducement of complete liquefaction.\n\n**Effects of Frequency and Loading Parameters:**\n\nLower frequency and smaller amplitude of vibration were found to increase the time required to achieve a given pore water pressure in dense tailings. The tailing liquefaction time varied nonlinearly with the height from the base of the tailing dam, with an initial decrease followed by an increase, depending on vibration frequency and amplitude and tailing density.\n\n**Fine Granular Materials and Cyclic Loading:**\n\nUndrained cyclic triaxial tests have been performed to study excess pore water pressure generation in fine granular materials including tailings, with evaluation based on density conditions, number of cycles, and applied cyclic stress ratios. Excess pore water pressure accumulated over time due to cyclic loading, with accumulation significantly dependent on densities, cyclic stress ratio values, and material types.\n\n**Factors Influencing Liquefaction:**\n\nThe magnitude of cyclic stress or strain affects liquefaction risk—the larger the cyclic stress or strain the fewer the number of cycles required to induce liquefaction."],"durationSeconds":10.187956332999995,"searchCount":1}}
