{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NLDZnfGvUpTQRVZWHmG8pM","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Arias intensity formula definition 1970 geotechnical earthquake engineering measure\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"INTERNATIONAL SOCIETY FOR SOIL MECHANICS AND GEOTECHNICAL ENGINEERING\",\"url\":\"https://www.issmge.org/uploads/publications/59/104/ch401.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Earthquake stress drop and Arias Intensity | U.S. Geological Survey\",\"url\":\"https://www.usgs.gov/publications/earthquake-stress-drop-and-arias-intensity\"},{\"title\":\"Arias intensity - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arias_intensity\"},{\"title\":\"Earthquake stress drop and Arias Intensity\",\"url\":\"https://pubs.usgs.gov/publication/70203381\"},{\"title\":\"Arias intensity — Grokipedia\",\"url\":\"https://grokipedia.com/page/arias_intensity\"},{\"title\":\"Directionality of Arias intensity in the Wenchuan Mw 7.9 earthquake, China - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0013795224001303\"},{\"title\":\"Paper Number 094 A closer look at Arias intensity-based liquefaction evaluation\",\"url\":\"https://www.nzsee.org.nz/db/2003/Print/Paper094p.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Earthquake Stress Drop and Arias Intensity - Baltay - 2019 - Journal of Geophysical Research: Solid Earth - Wiley Online Library\",\"url\":\"https://agupubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1029/2018JB016753\"},{\"title\":\"1 FINAL TECHNICAL REPORT AWARD NUMBER: G15AP00020 and G15AP00021\",\"url\":\"https://earthquake.usgs.gov/cfusion/external_grants/reports/G15AP00021.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Correlation of Arias intensity with amplitude, duration and cumulative intensity measures - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0267726115001827\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's information about the Arias intensity formula and its definition in geotechnical earthquake engineering:\n\n## Definition\n\nArias Intensity (Ia; Arias 1970) is an informative scalar ground-motion intensity measure that captures simultaneously multiple characteristics of a ground-motion recording such as amplitude, frequency content, and duration. Arias intensity is an engineering measure proportional to the integral of the absolute value of acceleration squared, over the significant duration of the signal.\n\n## The Formula\n\nThe Arias intensity is mathematically expressed as I_a = (π/2g) ∫[a(t)]² dt, where a(t) is the ground acceleration, g is gravitational acceleration, and t_d is the duration of significant motion; this parameter has units of velocity (e.g., m/s).\n\n## Origins and Purpose\n\nIt was proposed by Chilean engineer Arturo Arias in 1970. Arias intensity was originally proposed to provide an instrumental, energy-based alternative to subjective intensity scales like the Modified Mercalli Intensity, emphasizing its proportionality to the destructive capacity of ground motions on structures.\n\n## Applications\n\nArias intensity is a useful indicator of damage potential for a range of civil engineering structures and is commonly used in performance-based earthquake engineering applications. It has been found to be a fairly reliable parameter to describe earthquake shaking necessary to trigger landslides.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7d52a3ef-833c-4d3d-a19f-380d6d5297be","uuid":"e2479ebc-3dc2-432a-bd72-eac659f6db9d","timestamp":"2026-07-06T17:11:29.421Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"Arias intensity formula definition 1970 geotechnical earthquake engineering measure","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01BWEhMPnKKzGJGjuiG2987f","content":[{"title":"INTERNATIONAL SOCIETY FOR SOIL MECHANICS AND GEOTECHNICAL ENGINEERING","url":"https://www.issmge.org/uploads/publications/59/104/ch401.pdf"},{"title":"Earthquake stress drop and Arias Intensity | U.S. Geological Survey","url":"https://www.usgs.gov/publications/earthquake-stress-drop-and-arias-intensity"},{"title":"Arias intensity - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arias_intensity"},{"title":"Earthquake stress drop and Arias Intensity","url":"https://pubs.usgs.gov/publication/70203381"},{"title":"Arias intensity — Grokipedia","url":"https://grokipedia.com/page/arias_intensity"},{"title":"Directionality of Arias intensity in the Wenchuan Mw 7.9 earthquake, China - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0013795224001303"},{"title":"Paper Number 094 A closer look at Arias intensity-based liquefaction evaluation","url":"https://www.nzsee.org.nz/db/2003/Print/Paper094p.pdf"},{"title":"Earthquake Stress Drop and Arias Intensity - Baltay - 2019 - Journal of Geophysical Research: Solid Earth - Wiley Online Library","url":"https://agupubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1029/2018JB016753"},{"title":"1 FINAL TECHNICAL REPORT AWARD NUMBER: G15AP00020 and G15AP00021","url":"https://earthquake.usgs.gov/cfusion/external_grants/reports/G15AP00021.pdf"},{"title":"Correlation of Arias intensity with amplitude, duration and cumulative intensity measures - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0267726115001827"}]},"Based on the search results, here's information about the Arias intensity formula and its definition in geotechnical earthquake engineering:\n\n## Definition\n\nArias Intensity (Ia; Arias 1970) is an informative scalar ground-motion intensity measure that captures simultaneously multiple characteristics of a ground-motion recording such as amplitude, frequency content, and duration. Arias intensity is an engineering measure proportional to the integral of the absolute value of acceleration squared, over the significant duration of the signal.\n\n## The Formula\n\nThe Arias intensity is mathematically expressed as I_a = (π/2g) ∫[a(t)]² dt, where a(t) is the ground acceleration, g is gravitational acceleration, and t_d is the duration of significant motion; this parameter has units of velocity (e.g., m/s).\n\n## Origins and Purpose\n\nIt was proposed by Chilean engineer Arturo Arias in 1970. Arias intensity was originally proposed to provide an instrumental, energy-based alternative to subjective intensity scales like the Modified Mercalli Intensity, emphasizing its proportionality to the destructive capacity of ground motions on structures.\n\n## Applications\n\nArias intensity is a useful indicator of damage potential for a range of civil engineering structures and is commonly used in performance-based earthquake engineering applications. It has been found to be a fairly reliable parameter to describe earthquake shaking necessary to trigger landslides."],"durationSeconds":9.643720291999998,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Bgz4ujU6fmAQvrSmoE2FAX","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"dominant frequency bandwidth cycle count blast record pore pressure fine tailings selection criteria\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Excess pore water pressure generation in fine granular materials under undrained cyclic triaxial loading | International Journal of Geo-Engineering | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s40703-023-00185-y\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) PORE PRESSURE PREDICTION BASED ON SEISMIC ATTRIBUTES RESPONSE TO OVERPRESSURE\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/319220126_PORE_PRESSURE_PREDICTION_BASED_ON_SEISMIC_ATTRIBUTES_RESPONSE_TO_OVERPRESSURE\"},{\"title\":\"Screening of seismic records to perform time-history dynamic analyses of   tailings dams: a power-spectral based approach\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2009.06426\"},{\"title\":\"Research on Flocculant Selection for Classified Fine Tailings Based on Micro-Characterization of Floc Structure Characteristics\",\"url\":\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8999852/\"},{\"title\":\"Optimization of Dominant Frequency and Bandwidth Analysis in Multi-Frequency 3D GPR Signals to Identify Contaminated Areas\",\"url\":\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9784888/\"},{\"title\":\"of blasting on pore pressure\",\"url\":\"https://files.dep.state.pa.us/Mining/BureauOfMiningPrograms/BMPPortalFiles/Blasting_Research_Papers/Slope%20Stability/BoR%20Blasting%20and%20dams.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"A high-fidelity seismic intensity measure to assess dynamic liquefaction   in tailings\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2204.11393\"}]\n\nI've performed the web search for your query. The search returned relevant results covering several of the key topics in your search terms:\n\n**Key Findings:**\n\n1. **Dominant Frequency and Bandwidth**: In reflection seismology, important average measures include center frequency, spectral bandwidth, and dominant frequency, where dominant frequency is the square root of the second moment, serving as an estimate of the zero-crossing frequency.\n\n2. **Pore Pressure and Fine Tailings**: Studies on undrained cyclic triaxial tests examined excess pore water pressure generation in railway sand and tailings, with the response evaluated in terms of density conditions, number of cycles, and applied cyclic stress ratios.\n\n3. **Fine Tailings Selection Criteria**: The selection of a reasonable flocculant is essential for accelerating settlement speed of classified fine tailings, with analysis conducted through static sedimentation experiments, NMR observation, and electron microscope scanning.\n\n4. **Blast Records and Pore Pressure**: Blasting in or near earthfill dams can significantly increase residual pore-water pressure and reduce dam stability.\n\n5. **Seismic Analysis for Tailings**: Tailings dams are insensitive to higher frequency energies, with the most demanding seismic records being those with higher spectral power in lower frequencies.\n\nThese results cover aspects of your search across geotechnical engineering, seismic analysis, and tailings management.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7d52a3ef-833c-4d3d-a19f-380d6d5297be","uuid":"e26ea37e-2abc-4ac5-97f2-187e51f7b47d","timestamp":"2026-07-06T17:11:29.561Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"dominant frequency bandwidth cycle count blast record pore pressure fine tailings selection criteria","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01GLHqbouVKrCKnnqGJ5CH25","content":[{"title":"Excess pore water pressure generation in fine granular materials under undrained cyclic triaxial loading | International Journal of Geo-Engineering | Springer Nature Link","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s40703-023-00185-y"},{"title":"(PDF) PORE PRESSURE PREDICTION BASED ON SEISMIC ATTRIBUTES RESPONSE TO OVERPRESSURE","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/319220126_PORE_PRESSURE_PREDICTION_BASED_ON_SEISMIC_ATTRIBUTES_RESPONSE_TO_OVERPRESSURE"},{"title":"Screening of seismic records to perform time-history dynamic analyses of   tailings dams: a power-spectral based approach","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2009.06426"},{"title":"Research on Flocculant Selection for Classified Fine Tailings Based on Micro-Characterization of Floc Structure Characteristics","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8999852/"},{"title":"Optimization of Dominant Frequency and Bandwidth Analysis in Multi-Frequency 3D GPR Signals to Identify Contaminated Areas","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9784888/"},{"title":"of blasting on pore pressure","url":"https://files.dep.state.pa.us/Mining/BureauOfMiningPrograms/BMPPortalFiles/Blasting_Research_Papers/Slope%20Stability/BoR%20Blasting%20and%20dams.pdf"},{"title":"A high-fidelity seismic intensity measure to assess dynamic liquefaction   in tailings","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2204.11393"}]},"I've performed the web search for your query. The search returned relevant results covering several of the key topics in your search terms:\n\n**Key Findings:**\n\n1. **Dominant Frequency and Bandwidth**: In reflection seismology, important average measures include center frequency, spectral bandwidth, and dominant frequency, where dominant frequency is the square root of the second moment, serving as an estimate of the zero-crossing frequency.\n\n2. **Pore Pressure and Fine Tailings**: Studies on undrained cyclic triaxial tests examined excess pore water pressure generation in railway sand and tailings, with the response evaluated in terms of density conditions, number of cycles, and applied cyclic stress ratios.\n\n3. **Fine Tailings Selection Criteria**: The selection of a reasonable flocculant is essential for accelerating settlement speed of classified fine tailings, with analysis conducted through static sedimentation experiments, NMR observation, and electron microscope scanning.\n\n4. **Blast Records and Pore Pressure**: Blasting in or near earthfill dams can significantly increase residual pore-water pressure and reduce dam stability.\n\n5. **Seismic Analysis for Tailings**: Tailings dams are insensitive to higher frequency energies, with the most demanding seismic records being those with higher spectral power in lower frequencies.\n\nThese results cover aspects of your search across geotechnical engineering, seismic analysis, and tailings management."],"durationSeconds":9.017235791,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0136yqaHGtWreCPP4tavcsYd","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"CANLEX blast pore pressure Rollins Ashford field experiment liquefaction measurement\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Field Testing of Ground Improvement Techniques For Liquefaction Mitigation\",\"url\":\"https://www.iitk.ac.in/nicee/wcee/article/WCEE2012_0792.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Canadian Liquefaction Experiment (CANLEX): Blast-Induced Ground Motion and Pore Pressure Experiments | Semantic Scholar\",\"url\":\"https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Canadian-Liquefaction-Experiment-(CANLEX):-Ground-Al-Qasimi-Charlie/796b62679953a6bf17957321ef6a1a8bf93b05ba\"},{\"title\":\"Blast-Induced Pore Pressure and Liquefaction of Saturated Sand | Journal of Geotechnical and Geoenvironmental Engineering | Vol 139, No 8\",\"url\":\"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/abs/10.1061/(ASCE)GT.1943-5606.0000846\"},{\"title\":\"Pore Pressure Measurement in Blast-Induced Liquefaction Experiments - Kyle M. Rollins, J. Dusty Lane, Emily Dibb, Scott A. Ashford, A. Gray Mullins, 2005\",\"url\":\"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/0361198105193600124\"},{\"title\":\"Canadian Liquefaction Experiment (CANLEX): Blast-Induced Ground Motion and Pore Pressure Experiments\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/228670098_Canadian_Liquefaction_Experiment_CANLEX_Blast-Induced_Ground_Motion_and_Pore_Pressure_Experiments\"},{\"title\":\"INTERNATIONAL SOCIETY FOR SOIL MECHANICS AND GEOTECHNICAL ENGINEERING\",\"url\":\"https://www.issmge.org/uploads/publications/89/82/2_2_3.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Groundwater table mounding, pore pressure, and liquefaction induced by explosions: Energy‐distance relations - Charlie - 2007 - Reviews of Geophysics - Wiley Online Library\",\"url\":\"https://agupubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1029/2006RG000205\"},{\"title\":\"Recovery of small-strain stiffness following blast-induced liquefaction based on shear wave velocity measurements\",\"url\":\"https://cdnsciencepub.com/doi/abs/10.1139/cgj-2019-0658\"}]\n\nI found relevant information about this research topic. Here's what the search results show:\n\n## Overview\n\nA major aim of the Canadian Liquefaction Experiment (CANLEX) was to verify analysis procedures for predicting liquefaction phenomena. The Canadian Liquefaction Experiment (Canlex) involved Blast Induced Ground Motion and Pore Pressure Experiments.\n\n## Field Experiment Focus\n\nThe blasting experiments were conducted and analyzed to determine blast-induced ground motion and pore pressure response in a level deposit and to determine the possibility of using explosives to trigger flow-liquefaction in tailings located below an embankment, while keeping peak particle velocities within limits.\n\n## Key Researchers and Publications\n\nAshford, S. A., Rollins, K. M., and Lane, J. D. published work on \"Blast-induced liquefaction for full-scale foundation testing\" in the Journal of Geotechnical and Geoenvironmental Engineering in 2004.\n\n## Pore Pressure Measurement Challenges\n\nIn this extreme environment, pore pressure transducers must survive transient blast pressures of 41.4 MPa (6,000 psi) yet have enough resolution to measure residual pore pressures of ±0.69 kPa (0.1 psi). The piezoresistive transducer was found to be the most robust, and these sensors were repeatedly subjected to extreme blast pressures and vibration, but they still provided accurate time histories of residual pore pressure.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7d52a3ef-833c-4d3d-a19f-380d6d5297be","uuid":"c3c5750f-3fa3-4757-8639-a10e129c1c88","timestamp":"2026-07-06T17:11:30.218Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"CANLEX blast pore pressure Rollins Ashford field experiment liquefaction measurement","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01Ppd72erYpaTin7gZLnNjsx","content":[{"title":"Field Testing of Ground Improvement Techniques For Liquefaction Mitigation","url":"https://www.iitk.ac.in/nicee/wcee/article/WCEE2012_0792.pdf"},{"title":"Canadian Liquefaction Experiment (CANLEX): Blast-Induced Ground Motion and Pore Pressure Experiments | Semantic Scholar","url":"https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Canadian-Liquefaction-Experiment-(CANLEX):-Ground-Al-Qasimi-Charlie/796b62679953a6bf17957321ef6a1a8bf93b05ba"},{"title":"Blast-Induced Pore Pressure and Liquefaction of Saturated Sand | Journal of Geotechnical and Geoenvironmental Engineering | Vol 139, No 8","url":"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/abs/10.1061/(ASCE)GT.1943-5606.0000846"},{"title":"Pore Pressure Measurement in Blast-Induced Liquefaction Experiments - Kyle M. Rollins, J. Dusty Lane, Emily Dibb, Scott A. Ashford, A. Gray Mullins, 2005","url":"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/0361198105193600124"},{"title":"Canadian Liquefaction Experiment (CANLEX): Blast-Induced Ground Motion and Pore Pressure Experiments","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/228670098_Canadian_Liquefaction_Experiment_CANLEX_Blast-Induced_Ground_Motion_and_Pore_Pressure_Experiments"},{"title":"INTERNATIONAL SOCIETY FOR SOIL MECHANICS AND GEOTECHNICAL ENGINEERING","url":"https://www.issmge.org/uploads/publications/89/82/2_2_3.pdf"},{"title":"Groundwater table mounding, pore pressure, and liquefaction induced by explosions: Energy‐distance relations - Charlie - 2007 - Reviews of Geophysics - Wiley Online Library","url":"https://agupubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1029/2006RG000205"},{"title":"Recovery of small-strain stiffness following blast-induced liquefaction based on shear wave velocity measurements","url":"https://cdnsciencepub.com/doi/abs/10.1139/cgj-2019-0658"}]},"I found relevant information about this research topic. Here's what the search results show:\n\n## Overview\n\nA major aim of the Canadian Liquefaction Experiment (CANLEX) was to verify analysis procedures for predicting liquefaction phenomena. The Canadian Liquefaction Experiment (Canlex) involved Blast Induced Ground Motion and Pore Pressure Experiments.\n\n## Field Experiment Focus\n\nThe blasting experiments were conducted and analyzed to determine blast-induced ground motion and pore pressure response in a level deposit and to determine the possibility of using explosives to trigger flow-liquefaction in tailings located below an embankment, while keeping peak particle velocities within limits.\n\n## Key Researchers and Publications\n\nAshford, S. A., Rollins, K. M., and Lane, J. D. published work on \"Blast-induced liquefaction for full-scale foundation testing\" in the Journal of Geotechnical and Geoenvironmental Engineering in 2004.\n\n## Pore Pressure Measurement Challenges\n\nIn this extreme environment, pore pressure transducers must survive transient blast pressures of 41.4 MPa (6,000 psi) yet have enough resolution to measure residual pore pressures of ±0.69 kPa (0.1 psi). The piezoresistive transducer was found to be the most robust, and these sensors were repeatedly subjected to extreme blast pressures and vibration, but they still provided accurate time histories of residual pore pressure."],"durationSeconds":9.082033291999993,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0152TT4QUUctG1W3zo3Nc2oT","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"high fidelity intensity measure tailings liquefaction Fourier spectra response spectra blast\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"A high-fidelity seismic intensity measure to assess dynamic liquefaction   in tailings\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2204.11393\"},{\"title\":\"Ground Motion Intensity Measures for Liquefaction Hazard Evaluation | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/238439703_Ground_Motion_Intensity_Measures_for_Liquefaction_Hazard_Evaluation\"},{\"title\":\"Schematic diagram of digital pressure volume controller (DPVC) | Download Scientific Diagram\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Schematic-diagram-of-digital-pressure-volume-controller-DPVC_fig4_393172899\"},{\"title\":\"Screening of seismic records to perform time-history dynamic analyses of   tailings dams: a power-spectral based approach\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2009.06426\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Response Spectra at Liquefaction Sites during Shallow Crustal Earthquakes\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/270626349_Response_Spectra_at_Liquefaction_Sites_during_Shallow_Crustal_Earthquakes\"},{\"title\":\"Intensity measures for seismic liquefaction hazard evaluation of sloping site | Journal of Central South University\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11771-015-2944-5\"},{\"title\":\"Runout of liquefaction-induced tailings dam failure: Influence of   earthquake motions and residual strength\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.12727\"}]\n\nI found several highly relevant research articles and studies on this topic. Here are the key results:\n\n## Primary Finding\n\nA new high-fidelity seismic intensity measure (IM) has been developed to perform more accurate ground motion selection, which captures the spectral properties of the record for the frequency content that the dam does not filter. This IM was applied to a finite element model for an upstream tailings dam cross-section using a constitutive model capable of capturing dynamic liquefaction, and the results showed highly reliable correlations with different selected demands.\n\n## Related Research Areas\n\nGround motion characteristics of peak ground velocity (PGV) and Modified Acceleration Spectrum Intensity (MASI) show a strong correlation to the induced depth of liquefaction because they capture both the intensity and frequency content of the earthquake motion.\n\nA spectral power intensity measure showed improved results with an R² = 0.81 when the spectral power is accumulated in the 0-5.0 Hz window, confirming that higher demand correlates with higher power content in low and medium to low frequency ranges.\n\n## Blast-Induced Liquefaction Studies\n\nIn studies of controlled blast-induced liquefaction tests, particular attention was paid to the reproduction of acceleration records, velocities, Arias intensity, Fourier spectra, with modeling challenges including high frequencies in acceleration records exceeding 100 Hz and significant increases of excess pore water pressure over milliseconds.\n\nThese studies emphasize the importance of spectral characteristics and intensity measures for predicting liquefaction behavior in tailings dams under dynamic loading conditions.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7d52a3ef-833c-4d3d-a19f-380d6d5297be","uuid":"d14424bc-80c1-4108-984d-64fcdcb8a7e0","timestamp":"2026-07-06T17:11:30.907Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"high fidelity intensity measure tailings liquefaction Fourier spectra response spectra blast","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01XiDpg91JXNDuhVXtRh6PdA","content":[{"title":"A high-fidelity seismic intensity measure to assess dynamic liquefaction   in tailings","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2204.11393"},{"title":"Ground Motion Intensity Measures for Liquefaction Hazard Evaluation | Request PDF","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/238439703_Ground_Motion_Intensity_Measures_for_Liquefaction_Hazard_Evaluation"},{"title":"Schematic diagram of digital pressure volume controller (DPVC) | Download Scientific Diagram","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Schematic-diagram-of-digital-pressure-volume-controller-DPVC_fig4_393172899"},{"title":"Screening of seismic records to perform time-history dynamic analyses of   tailings dams: a power-spectral based approach","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2009.06426"},{"title":"(PDF) Response Spectra at Liquefaction Sites during Shallow Crustal Earthquakes","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/270626349_Response_Spectra_at_Liquefaction_Sites_during_Shallow_Crustal_Earthquakes"},{"title":"Intensity measures for seismic liquefaction hazard evaluation of sloping site | Journal of Central South University","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11771-015-2944-5"},{"title":"Runout of liquefaction-induced tailings dam failure: Influence of   earthquake motions and residual strength","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.12727"}]},"I found several highly relevant research articles and studies on this topic. Here are the key results:\n\n## Primary Finding\n\nA new high-fidelity seismic intensity measure (IM) has been developed to perform more accurate ground motion selection, which captures the spectral properties of the record for the frequency content that the dam does not filter. This IM was applied to a finite element model for an upstream tailings dam cross-section using a constitutive model capable of capturing dynamic liquefaction, and the results showed highly reliable correlations with different selected demands.\n\n## Related Research Areas\n\nGround motion characteristics of peak ground velocity (PGV) and Modified Acceleration Spectrum Intensity (MASI) show a strong correlation to the induced depth of liquefaction because they capture both the intensity and frequency content of the earthquake motion.\n\nA spectral power intensity measure showed improved results with an R² = 0.81 when the spectral power is accumulated in the 0-5.0 Hz window, confirming that higher demand correlates with higher power content in low and medium to low frequency ranges.\n\n## Blast-Induced Liquefaction Studies\n\nIn studies of controlled blast-induced liquefaction tests, particular attention was paid to the reproduction of acceleration records, velocities, Arias intensity, Fourier spectra, with modeling challenges including high frequencies in acceleration records exceeding 100 Hz and significant increases of excess pore water pressure over milliseconds.\n\nThese studies emphasize the importance of spectral characteristics and intensity measures for predicting liquefaction behavior in tailings dams under dynamic loading conditions."],"durationSeconds":9.054578999999999,"searchCount":1}}
