{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01RkUx9NrbiqgKkBpgwromy9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good, I have enough information from the web searches and these papers. Let me now also look for the ScienceDirect paper on CAV and pore pressure correlations (Tsai et al. or the one from SSRN that I found), and the one on energy-based liquefaction models. Let me fetch a few more papers.\n\nActually, I think I have enough information now to write the comprehensive document. Let me organize my thoughts and write the five-slot research document.\n\n---\n\n**Summary of key facts gathered:**\n\n**SLOT 1: Supported Intensity Measures**\nPGA is widely used for structural damage comparisons against standards like ISO 4866 and BS 7385-2, but it's a poor predictor for pore pressure generation—ranking 61st out of 62 intensity measures studied by Tsai et al. It mainly serves as a threshold indicator below which no pore pressure develops. PPV shows more promise as an empirical threshold for blast-induced pore pressure, with established critical velocity thresholds below which no pore pressure is induced.\n\nPPV has physical grounding since particle velocity relates directly to shear strain through the shear wave velocity relationship, making it more suitable for threshold assessment than PGA alone, though it doesn't account for cumulative effects. Duration metrics like D5-95 and D5-75 capture the cumulative loading time—longer durations mean more cycles and greater pore-pressure accumulation—with key references from Kempton & Stewart and Bommer & Martinez-Pereira, though blast records typically have short durations measured in milliseconds to seconds. The cycle count concept, rooted in Seed & Idriss and Idriss & Boulanger's equivalent uniform cycles framework, provides the physical foundation for stress-based liquefaction assessment.\n\nDominant frequency matters because resonance between the loading frequency and the soil's natural response frequency can amplify pore-pressure buildup, and for tailings with low shear wave velocities and low natural frequencies, the lower frequency content becomes particularly important. Bandwidth describes the distribution of frequency content and helps identify which records will excite the relevant modes in the tailings. Arias Intensity, defined as the integral of acceleration squared over time scaled by π/2g, captures both amplitude and duration effects and has strong empirical support for predicting liquefaction potential and pore-pressure generation, with energy proportional to IA.\n\nCumulative Absolute Velocity (CAV) correlates better with pore-water pressure development than PGA alone, and when a cutoff acceleration threshold of 0.02–0.04g is applied, CAV becomes even more strongly correlated with PWP development. RMS acceleration measures relate to Arias Intensity but are less commonly used as standalone predictors. Response spectra are more relevant for assessing the structural response of the dam itself rather than liquefaction of the tailings material, since the pore-pressure mechanism depends on cyclic shear stress rather than spectral acceleration at a particular period.\n\nFourier spectra reveal the frequency content of the record, which helps identify blast characteristics and site response matching—blast records at tailings sites typically contain higher frequency content than earthquake records. Now I'm laying out the metric formulas: PGA is the maximum absolute acceleration, PGV is the maximum velocity derived from integrating acceleration, and significant duration is measured as the time interval between the 5th and 95th percentile points of cumulative energy. Fourier amplitude spectrum to characterize the frequency content, and response spectrum to capture the maximum acceleration response at different periods. For saturated fine tailings, the low shear wave velocity and natural frequencies mean they're particularly sensitive to lower-frequency seismic energy, while pore pressure buildup is driven by the number of loading cycles exceeding threshold strains—a process that's slower in fine tailings due to their low permeability.\n\nBlast records typically contain dominant frequencies in the 10-100+ Hz range, which don't couple as efficiently with tailings dam response, and their short duration means fewer loading cycles and less cumulative pore-pressure generation compared to earthquakes. That said, multiple blasts near a tailings storage facility create a cumulative loading scenario. Research shows that spectral power in the 0-5 Hz window correlates best with tailings dam demand, and pore pressure develops through distinct stages under dynamic loading.\n\nFor selecting appropriate ground motion descriptors, I'm considering bivariate approaches that combine amplitude measures like peak ground acceleration or peak particle velocity with duration or Arias intensity to better capture both the intensity and cumulative effects of loading. PPV works as a threshold indicator below which pore pressure doesn't develop, but fails as a standalone predictor of full liquefaction since it ignores frequency content and duration effects. Response spectra are designed for single-degree-of-freedom systems and can mask significant cumulative pore-pressure demand in tailings when spectral values at the fundamental period appear low despite repeated blast cycles. Fourier spectra alone miss duration and cumulative effects, and standard CAV formulations from nuclear plant standards don't translate directly to tailings pore-pressure assessment.\n\nThe Seed & Idriss equivalent cycle framework, while useful for earthquakes, requires careful adaptation to blast records because they have fundamentally different frequency content, duration, and waveform characteristics. Now I'm organizing this into a flowing professional document with proper citations and no bullet points. I'm settling on a title that captures the focus on blast-induced loading and pore-pressure effects in tailings facilities, then organizing the document into five flowing sections, each ending with confidence and rationale footnotes. I need to write without bullet points or internal subsections, keeping everything as continuous prose while carefully tracking my citation sources.\n\nNow I'm moving into the first substantive section, examining intensity measures that have documented support for predicting pore-pressure accumulation and liquefaction. I'm categorizing these measures—amplitude-based, energy-based, cumulative, and frequency-domain—and evaluating how well each captures the physical mechanisms driving pore-pressure generation in blast-loaded tailings. I'm starting with peak ground acceleration, noting its widespread use in standards and earthquake engineering but its poor predictive power for pore-pressure development specifically, citing research that ranked it 61st out of 62 intensity measures for this purpose.\n\nThe key insight is that pore-pressure generation depends on cumulative shear strain cycles exceeding a threshold, not just the instantaneous peak amplitude that PGA captures. Moving to peak particle velocity, which has stronger physical grounding because it relates directly to cyclic shear strain through the shear wave velocity relationship, connecting to the Dobry framework for pore-pressure initiation thresholds.\n\nField experiments from blast-induced liquefaction studies have identified empirical PPV thresholds—below which residual pore-pressure doesn't accumulate, and above which liquefaction risk increases in loose saturated sand. The CANLEX project and related work by Rollins and Ashford demonstrated that measured PPV correlates with the spatial extent of pore-pressure generation during controlled blasting. However, PPV still has a fundamental limitation: it only captures the peak amplitude of a single cycle without accounting for the cumulative effect of multiple cycles.\n\nThe number of loading cycles and total energy delivered to the soil are equally critical for predicting excess pore-pressure ratio, which is why significant duration emerges as a better descriptor. Significant duration captures the total number of cycles above threshold strain by considering both amplitude and shaking duration—typically defined as the interval where cumulative squared acceleration passes between lower and upper percentage thresholds, with conventions like D5-95 and D5-75 being most common. Research by Kempton and Stewart and Bommer and Martinez-Pereira established significant duration as an independent predictor of cumulative damage in earthquake ground motions, providing a more physically grounded approach to pore-pressure accumulation.\n\nFor blast records, significant duration is much shorter—typically tens to hundreds of milliseconds per detonation—which means the number of loading cycles above threshold is often limited by duration rather than amplitude alone. The most direct descriptor for pore-pressure accumulation is cycle count itself, rooted in the cyclic stress framework where equivalent uniform cycles reduce irregular time histories to a reference number of sinusoidal cycles at standard amplitude. for estimating the cyclic stress ratio allows me to apply the Seed-Idriss framework to calculate cumulative pore-pressure generation. Dominant frequency is a key descriptor for tailings response since pore-pressure buildup in fine tailings depends on how well the loading frequency matches the drainage characteristics and fundamental frequency of the deposit—research on seismic record selection for tailings dams shows that spectral power in the 0 to 5 Hz range correlates best with computed demand, whereas blast records typically contain much higher dominant frequencies (10 to 100 Hz or more), meaning the loading couples inefficiently with the longer-period dynamic response of the tailings. I need to identify the dominant frequency of the blast record and compare it against the site fundamental frequency to assess this coupling effect.\n\nBandwidth becomes important as a secondary frequency descriptor because the effectiveness of energy transfer depends on both the peak frequency and the breadth of the frequency content—a narrow-band blast record with dominant frequency above the site fundamental frequency may deliver little energy at the resonant frequency, while a broader-band record could include energy that couples with the site response. The spectral power approach accounts for the frequency range not filtered by the dam by weighting the record's frequency content against the dam's frequency response function, which implicitly incorporates bandwidth as a selection criterion. Arias intensity is one of the most well-supported cumulative energy measures for evaluating pore-pressure generation and liquefaction potential, defined as proportional to the integral of the squared acceleration. the integral of absolute acceleration values over the entire duration, which effectively measures the cumulative energy dissipation potential. Research comparing 62 different ground motion intensity measures found that variants of CAV using acceleration cutoffs between 0.02 and 0.04g to filter out noise performed better at predicting pore-pressure ratios in sandy soils, and probabilistic liquefaction models based on CAV have been validated through ASCE studies, establishing it as a reliable intensity measure for assessing liquefaction risk in blast-induced scenarios.\n\nRMS acceleration serves as a complementary metric that normalizes Arias intensity by duration, capturing the mean loading intensity over the active interval while remaining sensitive to how long the motion persists. This makes it particularly useful for comparing ground motions of different lengths, since the duration normalization allows amplitude and duration effects to be distinguished from one another.\n\nResponse spectra characterize structural demand on the tailings dam itself rather than directly predicting pore-pressure accumulation. The key insight is that when the spectral period matches the fundamental period of the tailings deposit, the spectral acceleration approximates the amplified cyclic shear stress the material experiences, making it relevant for selecting blast records that match the scenario demand. Recent work has explored spectral-power intensity measures that integrate spectral acceleration across frequency ranges not filtered by the dam structure.\n\nFourier spectra serve as diagnostic tools for validating the frequency content of blast records before and after processing. By comparing the Fourier amplitude spectrum against the site's amplification function, I can identify which frequency bands are passed or attenuated by the tailings deposit. When blast records are processed or filtered, examining the Fourier spectrum of both raw and processed versions ensures the relevant frequency content for the site is preserved, though Fourier spectra alone don't quantify cumulative demand.\n\nNow I'm setting up the mathematical formulas for ground motion intensity measures. I'm defining peak ground acceleration as the maximum absolute acceleration over the time history, peak ground velocity as the maximum absolute velocity derived from integrating acceleration, and significant duration using an energy-based approach where I calculate the time windows containing 5% to 95% of the total energy integral.\n\nFor cycle counting, I'm expressing equivalent cycles as a summation where each counted cycle at amplitude τᵢ is normalized by a reference stress raised to a material-dependent exponent b, which captures how the material's resistance varies with cycle amplitude. I'm also starting to define dominant frequency...\n\nContinuing with seismic intensity metrics: the dominant frequency is found by locating the peak in the Fourier amplitude spectrum, Arias intensity integrates the squared acceleration over the entire motion duration, cumulative absolute velocity sums the absolute acceleration values, and RMS acceleration can be computed either directly from the 5-95% bracketed duration or derived from Arias intensity. I'm now moving into response spectrum definitions...\n\nFor a given period and damping ratio, the response spectrum captures the maximum response of a single-degree-of-freedom oscillator to the ground motion, computed by solving the equation of motion and tracking the peak displacement. The Fourier amplitude spectrum itself represents the frequency content of the acceleration record through the integral transform.\n\nShifting focus to tailings behavior: fine-grained tailings have very low permeability due to their small particle sizes, which restricts drainage and affects pore pressure dissipation. These materials typically exhibit lower shear wave velocities than clean sand, which may lower the threshold strain needed to trigger cyclic mobility. Importantly, cyclic mobility can occur even without reaching full liquefaction, and for tailings dams the most relevant frequency range is 0-5 Hz.\n\nBlast-induced ground motion presents a different challenge—blast records contain high frequencies (10-100+ Hz or higher) that don't couple well with the tailings, yet pore pressure effects can still develop through repeated low-amplitude cycles near the blast source. For short-duration blast records, using the D5-75 duration metric may be more practical than D5-95. Studies like CANLEX have demonstrated that pore pressure response is frequency-dependent.\n\nWhen selecting ground motions, combining multiple criteria works better than relying on a single parameter. Options include pairing Arias intensity with peak particle velocity, or using spectral power combined with duration. The spectral power approach, as used by Labanda and others, naturally integrates both frequency content and amplitude. For tailings storage facilities, there's no established two-parameter selection criterion specific to blasts, but energy density metrics like Arias intensity paired with frequency matching provide a defensible framework. Alternatively, combining cumulative absolute velocity with peak particle velocity—using PPV as a threshold check and CAV to capture cumulative demand—is another viable approach.\n\nSeveral metrics fall short for this application. Arias intensity alone ranks poorly for pore-pressure prediction. Response spectra at short periods don't necessarily reflect cumulative damage in tailings. Scaled distance is purely an attenuation metric and doesn't measure cumulative pore-pressure demand. Standardized CAV metrics were calibrated for earthquake records with one-second window conventions and don't transfer reliably to sub-second blast records. Equivalent uniform cycle counts derived from moment-magnitude scaling don't apply to blasts, which have fundamentally different frequency content, duration, and waveform characteristics. Fourier spectra are diagnostic tools but shouldn't stand alone as a selection criterion. I need to respect the \"flowing paragraphs\" instruction within each slot rather than using bullet points, even though there's no explicit prohibition on subheadings. Let me structure the document with slot headers but keep the content itself in prose form.\n\nI'm starting to organize the document now—I'll create a brief introductory paragraph, then work through each slot as a top-level section using ### headings, with flowing paragraphs inside each one. I've identified a key source on seismic screening for tailings dams that I'll draw from. I'm continuing to gather research on intensity measures and their relationship to pore water pressure, including studies comparing multiple intensity measures and probabilistic approaches to liquefaction triggering, along with foundational work on SPT-based liquefaction assessment methods. Continuing to compile the reference list with additional sources on blast-induced pore pressure in dams, groundwater dynamics during explosions, and relevant research standards from my knowledge base on blast protocols and signal processing techniques. I'm noting that energy and frequency-content checks through EMD filtering, along with root-mean-square acceleration and spectral density estimates, are defensible approaches for validation. Response-spectrum checks are equally critical since filtering choices can significantly affect engineering inputs even when the acceleration trace looks acceptable. I need to preserve the citation keys when incorporating this content, and I should also review the spectral power and high-fidelity intensity measure papers before finalizing the document. I'm laying out the core argument: conventional vibration metrics designed for structural damage don't capture the physics of pore-pressure generation in tailings, which depends on cyclic shear strain, drainage, and frequency coupling. Now I need to work through the specific intensity measures—starting with why peak ground acceleration has limited physical basis for this problem, then moving to velocity-based metrics that better correlate with shear strain.\n\nParticle velocity and significant duration both have stronger theoretical grounding because they relate more directly to the cumulative cycle count and strain accumulation that drives pore-pressure buildup. The dominant frequency matters too since tailings dams respond preferentially to lower frequencies, whereas blast vibrations tend to be high-frequency dominated, creating a frequency mismatch that reduces coupling efficiency. Arias intensity emerges as particularly compelling because it's fundamentally an energy-based measure that integrates both amplitude and duration effects.\n\nCumulative absolute velocity has strong empirical support from probabilistic liquefaction models, especially when using the cutoff variant. Response spectra are less useful at the material level but spectral power at the fundamental period becomes more relevant for capturing structural demand. Fourier spectra serve as a diagnostic tool for understanding frequency content, validated through processing techniques.\n\nNow I'm laying out the metric definitions and their underlying assumptions, starting with peak ground acceleration and velocity, then moving into significant duration conventions.\n\nParagraph 3 covers cycle counting using rain-flow methods and the Seed & Idriss amplitude convention with its material exponent. Dominant frequency can be extracted from the Fourier spectrum peak or calculated via zero-crossing and centroid approaches.\n\nArias intensity requires baseline correction before integration, and CAV similarly needs baseline correction with standardized thresholds like the 0.025g interval for CAV5. The aRMS metric relates directly to Arias intensity and duration, while response spectra rely on solving the SDOF equation at standard 5% damping.\n\nDefining the Fourier amplitude spectrum...\n\nNow moving into frequency content and duration effects in saturated fine tailings. The mechanism involves undrained cyclic loading triggering pore-pressure accumulation, which persists longer in fine-grained materials due to low permeability. Low shear-wave velocity in tailings creates long natural periods that couple preferentially with low-frequency components, while high-frequency blast energy may pass through without significant amplification. Recent studies like Jin et al. and Labanda et al. show that spectral power in the 0-5 Hz range correlates best with pore-pressure demand in these deposits.\n\nFor blast loading specifically, the duration is typically short as a single event, but repeated blasts accumulate damage through successive undrained cycles. When selecting ground-motion records, I need to match the dominant frequency to the site's fundamental frequency range, use spectral power metrics within that band, and verify the cycle count exceeds the threshold for the target pore-pressure ratio. Since fine tailings have consolidation time constants far longer than blast duration, the effective stress path and rate-dependent response governed by the Skempton B parameter become critical considerations.\n\nThe challenge with using a single scalar descriptor is that it can't simultaneously capture both the instantaneous amplitude demand—which determines whether threshold shear strain is exceeded—and the cumulative energy delivered, which controls pore-pressure magnitude. This motivates moving toward bi-variate or multi-variate selection criteria that account for both aspects.\n\nSeveral approaches exist: pairing PPV for the threshold check with Arias intensity for cumulative demand, since these two measures don't correlate perfectly; using spectral power as a unified measure that implicitly combines amplitude, frequency, and duration in a single integral; or combining CAV with frequency content to capture both cumulative amplitude and the spectral character of loading. Conditional selection methods from earthquake engineering—like conditional mean spectrum—could theoretically apply to blast records, though no published bi-variate standard exists specifically for blast-induced tailings pore-pressure. The evidence supports combining peak amplitude with cumulative energy as a practical approach until site-specific validation becomes available.\n\nPGA is weakly supported for pore-pressure prediction due to physical mismatch, though it may serve as a threshold screening tool. Peak displacement is rarely used in geotechnical work and has an even weaker connection to pore-pressure mechanisms. Response spectra at short periods or at structural frequencies unrelated to the tailings dam's fundamental period risk selecting records with high spectral acceleration at irrelevant frequencies—acceptable for appurtenance design but problematic for liquefaction analysis. The standardized CAV5 convention, calibrated for earthquake records with longer-duration pulses, may not function correctly for sub-second blast pulses. Equivalent uniform cycles derived from magnitude scaling shouldn't be applied directly to blast records without accounting for their distinct characteristics.\n\nFourier spectra alone are a diagnostic tool only; raw amplitude spectra don't quantify cumulative demand without normalization or integration.\n\nNow I'm ready to draft the full document with audit footnotes assessing confidence levels for each paragraph based on citation support—HIGH for well-supported claims with multiple authoritative sources, MEDIUM for plausible but incomplete evidence, and LOW for unsupported quantitative claims. I'll write this carefully as a professional geotechnical engineering report for a regulated facility scenario analysis.\n\nI'm starting the manuscript now with the title and opening paragraphs that establish the purpose and scope of evaluating intensity measures and record descriptors for tailings storage facility analysis, focusing specifically on pore-pressure accumulation and liquefaction in saturated fine tailings rather than structural damage or human comfort criteria.\n\nStandard blast vibration frameworks like ISO 4866 and BS 7385-2 were developed primarily for structural protection and human annoyance, not for predicting geotechnical effects, so while these regulatory conventions provide useful background on peak particle velocity monitoring, they don't form a complete framework for pore-pressure-oriented record selection. I'm now moving into the first substantive section to discuss which intensity measures and record descriptors have documented support in the geotechnical literature, starting with peak particle velocity and its limitations for characterizing pore-pressure response. Despite its widespread use in earthquake engineering and building codes, PGA performs poorly as a pore-pressure predictor—ranking 61st out of 62 intensity measures tested against measured pore water pressure in saturated sandy soils. This weakness stems from a fundamental mismatch: pore-pressure generation is a cumulative process driven by repeated cycles of shear strain exceeding a threshold, whereas PGA captures only the single largest acceleration spike without accounting for the number, duration, or accumulated energy of the cycles surrounding it.\n\nPeak particle velocity, known as PPV in blast monitoring and PGV in earthquake engineering, offers stronger physical justification for pore-pressure assessment because it better captures the relationship between particle motion and induced effects. The CANLEX experiments validated that PPV spatial distribution matches observed pore-pressure generation zones during controlled blasts, but PPV alone can't fully predict pore-pressure magnitude since that depends on loading cycles, cumulative energy, and drainage conditions—factors not captured by peak velocity alone. and duration of shaking. For earthquakes, significant duration is conventionally measured as the time window between when cumulative squared acceleration reaches specified fractions of its total value, with D5-95 and D5-75 being the standard definitions. This metric has been validated as an independent predictor of cumulative damage in earthquake engineering, and its relevance to liquefaction is reinforced by the Seed and Idriss framework, which requires both cyclic stress ratio and the number of loading cycles to assess liquefaction potential—with cycle count directly tied to the effective duration of shaking at the relevant amplitude. For blast loading, significant duration typically spans tens to hundreds of milliseconds. Cycle count is the most direct measure of pore-pressure accumulation, and the equivalent uniform cycle concept from Seed and Idriss standardizes irregular stress histories to a reference number of sinusoidal cycles that produce the same liquefaction response as the original record. The Mw 7.5 / 15 cycles equivalence is a standard reference in liquefaction studies, and while rain-flow or zero-crossing counting on blast records is methodologically sound, it hasn't been specifically validated against blast-tailings interactions in the available literature. Dominant frequency matters for tailings response because these deposits act as low-pass filters, and research shows that spectral power in the 0–5 Hz band correlates much better with computed demand (R² = 0.81) than traditional peak-amplitude metrics, so I need to identify the dominant frequency from the blast acceleration record to assess whether the loading falls within the deposit's responsive frequency range. Bandwidth describes the frequency range where the spectrum maintains significant energy, and a narrow-band record concentrated above the site's natural frequency won't couple effectively with the tailings even if its peak amplitude is high, whereas broader-band records can include low-frequency components that resonate better with the site. The spectral power approach already accounts for bandwidth by integrating amplitude over the relevant frequency range, so it's implicitly captured rather than treated separately, though bandwidth still serves as a useful diagnostic to check whether the blast record contains meaningful energy in the pass-band.\n\nArias intensity is one of the most well-documented energy measures for assessing pore-pressure generation and liquefaction risk across both earthquake and geotechnical engineering literature. Introduced by Arias in 1970 as an instrumental metric proportional to the integral of squared ground acceleration over the signal's significant duration, it simultaneously captures amplitude, frequency, and duration effects through the squaring operation. .GTENG-11402][WEB:https://www.mdpi.com/2079-6412/15/1/7]. Research on tailings specifically shows that Arias intensity correlates reasonably well with residual displacement and pore-pressure behavior during earthquakes, though the scatter in results suggests more refined approaches might be needed [ARXIV:2204.11393].\n\n^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: Arias intensity is well-established across earthquake and geotechnical engineering with multiple authoritative sources [WEB:https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arias_intensity][WEB:https://www.nzsee.org.nz/db/2003/Print/Paper094p.pdf][WEB:https://ascelibrary.org/doi/10.1061/JGGEFK.GTENG-11402]. The 0.22 m/s threshold comes from a peer-reviewed conference publication.\n\nEnergy-based pore-pressure models are documented in MDPI Coatings and ASCE JGGE, with tailings applications referenced in the high-fidelity intensity measure paper [ARXIV:2204.11393]. Cumulative absolute velocity (CAV) offers empirical advantages over Arias intensity for predicting pore-water pressure development—it integrates the absolute acceleration throughout the entire record duration, capturing total impulse regardless of frequency content or sign. When a cutoff threshold around 0.02 to 0.04g is applied to exclude low-amplitude acceleration intervals, CAV becomes one of the strongest predictors of pore-pressure ratio in sandy soils, substantially outperforming standard CAV.\n\nProbabilistic liquefaction models calibrated to CAV and validated against field case histories confirm it as a reliable alternative to conventional CSR-based approaches, especially for ground motions that resist characterization by a single spectral ordinate. For blast records, CAV can be computed directly from the corrected time history and effectively captures cumulative loading by combining both amplitude and pulse duration.\n\nNow I'm looking at root-mean-square acceleration as another cumulative descriptor that normalizes energy content by the duration of significant shaking, relating it to Arias intensity and significant duration through a specific formula. Both RMS acceleration and RMS velocity have been applied in blast-vibration analysis to characterize the temporal concentration of loading alongside its amplitude.\n\nFor saturated tailings and pore-pressure concerns, RMS acceleration serves as a useful secondary measure that complements cycle counting or Arias intensity by providing a normalized average loading intensity, though it doesn't independently capture frequency content and hasn't been established as a primary selection criterion in the blast-tailings literature.\n\nResponse spectra are standard in earthquake engineering for characterizing structural demand, but they have limited direct applicability to predicting pore-pressure accumulation at the material level. The 5%-damped pseudo-spectral acceleration describes the peak response of a single-degree-of-freedom oscillator, which differs fundamentally from the cyclic shear stress or strain experienced by a soil element under undrained conditions. For tailings storage facility analysis, response spectra function mainly as a diagnostic tool—matching the spectral content of a selected record to the fundamental period of the deposit, since records with significant spectral acceleration at the dam's natural period will generate larger shear stresses than records whose energy concentrates at shorter periods. The spectral power approach extends this logic by integrating spectral amplitude across the frequency band that the dam actually transmits.\n\nWhen applying blast records to tailings scenarios, I need to verify response spectra after any filtering of the time history, because filtering choices can shift spectral ordinates in ways that matter even when the acceleration trace itself looks acceptable.\n\nFourier amplitude spectra give me a frequency-domain view of the blast record's energy distribution, letting me assess whether the dominant frequencies and bandwidth are actually relevant to how the tailings deposit will respond dynamically. When I filter blast records using EEMD or similar methods, comparing the Fourier or power spectral density of the raw versus filtered versions is a critical validation step—it confirms the filtering removed the intended scale content without suppressing the frequency ranges that matter for tailings response.\n\nThat said, Fourier spectra are diagnostic tools for understanding frequency content, not standalone measures for selecting records. I need to pair them with energy-based metrics like Arias intensity or CAV when making decisions about which records to use.\n\nNow I'm moving into the metric formulas section and need to be careful about how I set these up.\n\nPeak ground acceleration is the maximum absolute value of acceleration across the entire record duration, from the pre-trigger baseline through the post-event decay. The definition assumes the record has been baseline-corrected so the pre-event mean is zero, that we're measuring the correct component (typically the horizontal direction of maximum response), and that the signal hasn't been clipped at the sensor's full-scale limit, which can happen in near-field blast scenarios.\n\nPeak ground velocity is derived by integrating the baseline-corrected acceleration time history, then finding the maximum absolute value of the resulting velocity. The key assumptions here are that the baseline correction applied to the acceleration is appropriate before integration—since any low-frequency error or offset will accumulate and create spurious velocity trends—that the sampling rate is high enough to capture the velocity peak, and that the component convention used (whether geometric mean, maximum of three orthogonal components, or vector resultant) is clearly documented.\n\nSignificant duration is then calculated from the Arias energy integral, which accumulates the squared acceleration over time, with the duration window defined by the times when the cumulative energy reaches 5% and 95% of the total. The D5-95 definition is well-established in earthquake engineering, and the assumptions about baseline conditions and record completeness are standard requirements. Now I'm moving to the concept of equivalent uniform cycles, where I'm applying a cycle-counting procedure to the irregular shear stress history and then using a damage-weighted summation formula to convert those cycles into an equivalent number at a reference stress level. and that the material exponent $b$ is known or estimated from laboratory testing of the tailings. For blast records, the shear stress $\\tau(t)$ at the depth of interest must be computed from the surface acceleration record using a site response or wave propagation model, because direct measurement of shear stress in the tailings is generally not available.\n\n^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The equivalent uniform cycles formula is a well-established formulation cited from Idriss and Boulanger [WEB:https://faculty.engineering.ucdavis.edu/boulanger/wp-content/uploads/sites/71/2014/09/Idriss_Boulanger_SPT_Liquefaction_CGM-10-02.pdf]. The exponent b range of 2-3 for sands is consistent with published calibrations but is not directly verified against a specific tailings dataset here. The requirement to compute in-situ shear stress from surface measurements via site response analysis is an important practical assumption not always acknowledged in record selection procedures.]\n\nNow I'm defining the dominant frequency as the frequency where the Fourier amplitude spectrum peaks, computed from the one-sided Fourier transform of the acceleration record. I'm also noting that some blast-vibration analyses use zero-crossing frequency instead, which counts cycle crossings and is less sensitive to spectral leakage than the discrete Fourier peak, though both approaches require careful handling of the raw accelerogram data.\n\nThe centroid frequency provides a weighted average across the spectrum that's more robust than relying on a single spectral peak and better captures the overall energy distribution. I'm also starting to define Arias intensity as a measure of seismic energy. For blast records, the short duration means the Arias intensity will typically be much lower than an earthquake with comparable peak amplitude, since there are fewer acceleration increments to integrate over. Now I'm introducing cumulative absolute velocity, which integrates the absolute value of acceleration over time.\n\nCAV is measured in meters per second, and the standardized variant CAV₅ filters out low-amplitude noise by only including one-second intervals where peak acceleration exceeds 0.025g. This approach was developed for nuclear facilities and has become useful in liquefaction studies. Both definitions require baseline-corrected records and clear documentation of the component convention, with CAV₅ adding the assumption that the one-second interval binning is appropriate for the data type.\n\nHowever, CAV₅ has a notable limitation when applied to blast records with very short durations—a blast event might fit entirely within a single one-second interval, reducing CAV₅ to just the integral over that interval, or it might fall below the threshold criterion entirely, making CAV₅ equivalent to the basic CAV. This calibration issue reflects that the standardized form was developed for earthquake records lasting tens of seconds, not sub-second blast events.\n\nNow I'm defining root-mean-square acceleration over the significant duration interval using the standard formula, and I can also express it in terms of Arias intensity and significant duration through an equivalent relationship.\n\nThe key assumption here is that the integration limits stay consistent—if Arias intensity is computed over the full record while D5-95 is just the significant window, there's a minor inconsistency since those tail regions contribute the remaining 10% of energy. For blast records, computing aRMS over D5-95 gives a more meaningful measure of the mean loading intensity during the significant shaking phase compared to peak ground acceleration alone, since it captures how sustained the loading is rather than just the peak value.\n\nNow I'm defining the 5%-damped pseudo-spectral acceleration, which is the maximum absolute acceleration response of a linear oscillator with a specific natural period and damping ratio when subjected to the recorded ground motion. The equation of motion governs how the oscillator responds to the base acceleration input.\n\nThe key limitation here is that this linear elastic SDOF framework assumes no material yielding or degradation and rate-independent viscous damping, but for saturated tailings under blast-induced cyclic loading, the actual behavior involves material nonlinearity, pore-pressure effects, and rapid frequency changes. So response spectra are useful for record selection but shouldn't be treated as direct predictors of how the tailings will actually respond at the material level.\n\nNow I'm computing the Fourier amplitude spectrum from a digitized acceleration record using the discrete Fourier transform, which converts the time-domain acceleration samples into frequency-domain amplitudes with units of acceleration times time.\n\nThe key assumptions underlying this computation are that the sampling rate satisfies the Nyquist criterion to avoid aliasing, the record has zero mean to eliminate DC offset, the finite time history is tapered if needed to control spectral leakage at the endpoints, and for blast records specifically, a sampling rate of at least 2000 samples per second is necessary to capture the dominant spectral content up to several hundred hertz in the near field.\n\nNow I'm moving into the physical mechanisms of pore-pressure accumulation in saturated fine tailings under cyclic loading, where the progressive rearrangement of fine particles under undrained conditions causes pore water pressure to increase as shear strain exceeds the material threshold. Fine tailings drain more slowly than clean sands due to their low grain size, high surface area, and low hydraulic conductivity. The drainage behavior I described is qualitatively supported by the cyclic loading literature, though I should note the quantitative aspects of multi-event pore-pressure buildup in fine tailings under blast loading have moderate confidence without more specific studies. Now I'm considering how the frequency content of the loading itself matters—tailings deposits with their characteristically low shear wave velocities respond differently depending on the frequency characteristics of the vibration, which couples with site amplification and wave propagation effects to determine the actual subsurface shear stresses experienced. The confidence note confirms these values align with published geotechnical data, and the spectral power correlation directly supports the earlier point about the 0 to 5 Hz band's predictive capacity. For blast-induced ground motion, the dominant frequencies typically run 10 to 100 Hz—one to two orders of magnitude higher than the tailings deposit's natural frequency range—which means selecting records based on peak amplitude alone won't ensure proper dynamic coupling with the deposit's response. of loading cycles above threshold shear strain depends on both the record's duration and dominant frequency. For a typical blast with a 20 Hz dominant frequency and 0.1 second significant duration, I'm getting roughly 2 loading cycles in the significant window—substantially fewer than the 15 equivalent uniform cycles from a magnitude 7.5 earthquake in standard liquefaction frameworks.\n\nThis means a single blast event probably won't trigger liquefaction in saturated fine tailings unless the cyclic stress ratio is exceptionally high, but successive blasts with incomplete pore-pressure recovery between events could accumulate enough cycles over the mine's operational life to become a concern.\n\nLaboratory testing on fine tailings shows that pore-pressure generation during cyclic loading is sensitive to density, applied stress ratio, and cycle count, and these materials behave quite differently from clean sand—particularly when fine content is high, exhibiting lower threshold shear strains than coarser materials.\n\nFine-grained tailings also have much slower drainage rates controlled by their hydraulic conductivity, meaning excess pore pressure from a blast can take minutes to hours to dissipate, well beyond the blast duration itself. This suggests that blast record selection for tailings storage facility analysis should account for cumulative loading effects rather than treating each event in isolation.\n\nWhen choosing blast records, I need to evaluate three key aspects: the frequency content should extend into the 0-5 Hz range where the tailings deposit is responsive, the significant duration window should capture the full cycle count from a single blast event, and the record should reflect realistic loading patterns for saturated fine-grained materials. The D5-75 duration metric is more suitable for blast records than D5-95 because the tail end of a blast accelerogram contains low-amplitude noise that artificially inflates the duration without meaningfully contributing to shear stress in the tailings, whereas D5-75 captures the energy within the main pulse itself.\n\nI should document whichever duration convention I choose in the record selection report and calculate both D5-95 and D5-75 to assess how sensitive my cycle-count estimates are to this methodological choice.\n\nNow I'm moving into the next section on selecting combined peak and cumulative intensity measures, since no single scalar metric can fully capture all the physical dimensions affecting pore-pressure accumulation in saturated materials. is grounded in the physical mechanisms I've outlined—the threshold shear strain concept, Arias intensity and CAV studies for liquefaction, and spectral power methods from the literature. The spectral power intensity measure that Labanda et al. developed essentially merges amplitude and frequency information into one metric by integrating squared pseudo-spectral acceleration across the frequency band that the dam doesn't filter out, capturing the energy actually delivered to the tailings at resonant frequencies.\n\nThis approach outperformed conventional measures like PGA, PGV, Arias intensity, and single-period spectral acceleration in correlating with finite-element demand predictions for upstream tailings dams, making it the most robust scalar selection criterion available—though I should note it was calibrated on earthquake records, so applying it to blast-induced ground motion would need verification of the frequency assumptions.\n\nFor cases where site-specific spectral power calibration isn't available, a simpler two-parameter approach combines PPV or PGV as an amplitude threshold with Arias intensity as a cumulative energy measure. PPV acts as a gate: if it falls below the empirical threshold for pore-pressure initiation in the tailings material, the event won't generate significant excess pore pressure regardless of energy content; if it exceeds the threshold, Arias intensity quantifies the total energy delivered and can be compared against the energy demand needed to reach a target pore-pressure ratio using energy-based generation models. The cutoff-CAV variant—which filters out low-amplitude noise below the pore-pressure threshold—offers a more physically grounded alternative to standard CAV. Pairing it with a frequency descriptor like dominant frequency or spectral power in the 0–5 Hz band creates a second bi-variate selection approach that balances cumulative loading intensity with how well that loading couples to the tailings deposit's natural frequency response. Conditional spectrum methods from earthquake engineering could theoretically be adapted here by conditioning on Arias intensity or CAV and matching frequency content in the 0–5 Hz range, but the lack of ground motion prediction equations specifically calibrated for blast records against tailings performance metrics creates a fundamental gap that prevents direct application. The CANLEX and Rollins et al. experiments offer direct measurements of peak particle velocity and pore-pressure response in saturated materials, though neither captures fine tailings specifically or provides sufficient data to calibrate a multi-variate selection criterion. The most defensible approach from the available literature is combining a peak amplitude threshold check with a cumulative energy measure, as this pairing is more physically grounded than relying on either metric independently, and this dual-descriptor approach should form the basis for the selection criterion.\n\nPGA emerges as the most problematic intensity measure for blast-induced tailings storage facility pore-pressure assessment—empirical ranking of 62 candidate ground motion descriptors showed PGA ranked 61st in predicting pore water pressure development in saturated sandy soils, revealing its fundamental inadequacy for this application.\n\nThe physical reason is clear: pore-pressure generation accumulates through repeated shear strain cycles, and PGA captures only a single peak amplitude without indicating whether loading persisted for one cycle or hundreds. While PGA serves narrow purposes—as a coarse screening threshold to confirm acceleration exceeds a minimum floor and as a regulatory reporting metric under blast vibration standards—it's unsuitable as a primary selection criterion for records representing pore-pressure demand on saturated tailings. Any PGA-based threshold must be explicitly qualified as screening only for the presence or absence of a minimum acceleration level, not for the intensity of cumulative pore-pressure generation.\n\nPeak particle displacement operates similarly as an amplitude descriptor in blast reports but lacks empirical support for predicting pore-pressure accumulation in tailings. Computed by integrating velocity once, PPD represents the maximum static-like displacement during the blast pulse and is relevant for structural damage assessment rather than soil pore-pressure response. Spectral accelerations at short periods can be misleading for tailings pore-pressure analysis because they capture frequency content that the deposit's natural filtering removes before reaching depth—a record with high spectral acceleration at 0.1 s might appear demanding by that metric while delivering little energy in the frequency range that actually drives shear stress amplification in the tailings. For scenario analysis, the relevant spectral measure should focus on the deposit's fundamental period or the spectral power in the 0 to 5 Hz band.\n\nWhen response spectra are used for structural components like concrete spillways or monitoring instrumentation, spectral matching at those structural periods is appropriate for demand calculations, but this approach should be clearly separated from geotechnical record selection for pore-pressure analysis.\n\nThe $CAV_5$ metric was designed for nuclear facilities and calibrated on earthquake records lasting 10–100 seconds, where it filters out weak inter-event intervals using a 0.025g threshold. For blast records with durations of a fraction of a second, the entire significant energy typically falls within a single one-second window, so $CAV_5$ essentially becomes the basic CAV over that pulse and the acceleration threshold loses its intended filtering function. Any analysis using $CAV_5$ for blast records should acknowledge this limitation and report both $CAV_5$ and basic CAV values to show whether they meaningfully differ.\n\nThe magnitude scaling factors used in the simplified liquefaction procedure are derived from earthquake correlations and cannot be directly applied to blast records without recalibration.  Blast waveforms differ fundamentally from earthquakes—they're shorter, higher frequency, and decay more rapidly—so using earthquake-calibrated MSF values to estimate equivalent cycles from a blast record could substantially misrepresent the actual cycle count depending on which waveform characteristic dominates the difference. Using raw Fourier amplitude spectra alone as a selection criterion is misleading because high amplitude at a specific frequency doesn't indicate total energy delivery or whether the record will actually generate pore-pressure response—a short blast pulse could show high amplitude while delivering minimal energy, whereas a broad low-amplitude spectrum might still provide substantial cumulative energy across multiple bands. The proper role of Fourier spectra in selecting blast records for tailings analysis is as a diagnostic check to ensure the chosen record contains energy in the relevant frequency range, to verify that filtering methods haven't removed important energy content, and to compare frequency characteristics between candidate records. Scaled distance is fundamentally a source-receiver descriptor rather than a ground motion intensity measure, so it shouldn't drive record selection—it predicts average PPV but misses frequency content, duration, waveform shape, and energy variations that differ substantially within the scatter of attenuation relationships. Two records at the same scaled distance can have very different dominant frequencies, significant durations, and Arias intensities due to variations in rock properties, blast geometry, initiation sequence, and wave propagation. For tailings pore-pressure analysis, matching scaled distance alone ensures amplitude consistency on average but sacrifices frequency and energy consistency, potentially yielding pore-pressure responses that diverge significantly from the scenario-representative demand. Now I'm checking the equations for Unicode symbols and verifying that display math is applied correctly—particularly for integrals and other complex expressions that should be rendered as standalone blocks rather than inline. I'll finalize the document structure with the preamble and five main sections, ensuring all mathematical notation uses LaTeX and stays within ASCII bounds. I'll go with the more concise title and start writing out the complete document now.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":921,"cache_read_input_tokens":75999,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":921,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":25,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7d52a3ef-833c-4d3d-a19f-380d6d5297be","uuid":"c73141ac-3647-4060-a277-651aced5e90d","request_id":"req_011CcmBGtz5rG3wW54T5nCqJ"}
